// The boss journal: Maelis Orne, the Pale Regent, keeper of the last house on the Silver Road, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Maelis never speaks, so there are no lines from her. Move keys are cast names (the journal fires on the cast bar's name).
// The idea every page carries: the crown behind her head is a small moon, and the lit part of it, turned the way the crown is turned, is the part of the round
// floor her next cut strikes. Moves come in the moon's own order (crescent, half, gibbous, full), so a dance can be read from its first cut. In Act V the rule
// turns over: the dark of the crown is what strikes. The paint on the floor is always true, and it always shows after the crown has.
// Moonrise is the opener of every act and carries the basic lesson (The Waxing and Hunter's Moon are the same light blast and are folded into it).
// Crescent, Half Moon and Gibbous are the single cuts, Full Moon the whole floor lit, Earthshine her cut at the knight and its pale second hit (and, from the
// saddle, the line she rides along where the knight has walked), Moonwell the shafts that want two heroes each, Moonlets the small moons that burst when a
// waxing dance ends. Waxing Dance and Waning Dance are the four-cut dances. New Moon is the wall, Moonless Steps its sword dance, The Last Light its gather.
// The Hound's Return, Hunt's Pass and Earthshine from the saddle are the Hunt: the rule is Garrow's (he goes where a trail goes, and hunts what runs).
// The Long Eclipse turns the rule over; Umbral Crescent, Umbral Half Moon and The Last Dance live under it; Totality is the enrage and has its own page.
// Two Crowns, Annular, Libration, Moonfall and Harvest are the Hard-only pages (hard: true).
// Moon-Touched, Blood Moon Dance and Blood Harvest are the Extreme-only pages (extreme: true). The Second Howl (Extreme: Garrow's shade runs the freshest trail in
// the middle of each Last Dance) is a short cast of its own, so it has a page too (extreme: true).
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).maelis = {
  // the first time this hero meets her (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "A tall woman, still as a gatepost, her robe lit down one side and black down the other. Behind her head floats a crown that is a small moon. What is lit on it is struck on the floor. Report: the room is round, and so is her question.",
    gloomsworn: "The lady of the house is a silver figure with a blade like a new moon, and a small moon turning behind her head. I have stood at the end of roads before. Here, what was bright on the crown was cut on the floor, and the dark was where the living stood.",
    thresher: "Such a tall, still hostess, dear, one half of her robe lit and the other dark, and a little moon floating behind her head for a crown. It shows her mind: whatever is bright on it will be cut on the floor. A lovely place to end a road.",
    bloodreaver: "At the end of the road stood the tallest woman I ever wanted to fight, a blade as long as she is, and a little moon hung behind her head! Whatever part of it shines, she cuts the same part of the floor. I got cut plenty learning it.",
    runeguard: "Maelis Orne, the Pale Regent: a robe lit on one side and black on the other, a crescent blade, and behind her head a thin crown that is a small moon. First entry in the margin: the bright part of the crown is the part of the round floor she cuts, turned the same way.",
    gorgemaw: "My hostess at the end of the road: tall, silver-haired, half her robe bright and half black, a crescent blade for a carving knife. Behind her head a small moon hung like a garnish. Whatever part was lit, that part of the floor was carved. I sat in the unlit portion.",
    oathbroker: "The Regent of the last house stands very tall and still, her robe lit upon one side and black upon the other, a small moon where a halo would be. By her own terms, the lit portion of that moon is the portion of the floor she will strike. The dark portion is exempt.",
    ironpalm: "She stood at the end of the road and said nothing. A blade like a new moon, and a thin moon turning behind her head. The lit part of it was the part of the floor she meant. I looked at the crown first and the floor after, and in that order I lived.",
    ranger: "Tall. Silver hair to her heels, robe lit down one side, black down the other, blade as long as she is. Small moon floating behind her head. What's lit on it is what she cuts on the floor, turned the same way. Wide room, long walks. Look up first.",
    shadowblade: "The mark is a very tall woman, half in lamplight and half in shadow, with a small moon behind her head. I felt a kinship with the dark half. The bright part of the crown is the part of the floor she cuts, so I took her shadow side as my own.",
    pyromancer: "A tall woman with a blade like a new moon and a little moon hung behind her head like a glowing circlet! Whatever part of it shone, the same part of the floor was struck. I had never been upstaged by a crown. I took the dark part of the room and finished my spell.",
    thermist: "Cold clean through, a long silver woman, half her robe bright and half black, a small moon behind her head. Bright on the crown means cut on the floor. I ran hot, then cold, learning which half of the room I stood in. The dark half is the cool one.",
    driftblade: "A woman as tall as a doorway, a blade like a new moon, a small moon turning behind her head, and a wide round floor under us all that she cut by the shape of it, bright side first, and I ran for the dark side every time, and it was a long way.",
    alembist: "Subject: one woman, very tall, robe lit on one side and black on the other, crescent blade, and a small moon behind her head that shows what she will do. Finding: the bright part of the crown is the part of the floor she cuts. The dark part is the control group. Stand there.",
    threadweaver: "A still lady in a robe cut half from light and half from shadow, a blade shaped like a new moon, and a little moon behind her head. The bright part of it is the part of the floor she cuts through. I took my stitches to the dark half and kept them there.",
    marionettist: "The last act, and what a set: a round pale floor under a black sky, and our leading lady very tall and still, with a moon for a crown. Whatever part of the crown is lit, that part of the stage is cut. (The Doll stood in the dark half, as understudies should.)",
    cleric: "Poor lady, so tall and so still, in a robe lit on one side and black on the other, with a small moon turning behind her head. I watched the crown and the floor and saw that bright on one is cut on the other. I told everyone to look up before they looked down.",
    grovekeeper: "A tall pale woman in the middle of a bare grey field, a small moon hung behind her head, as if she had carried the night's own weather in with her. What is bright on the crown is cut on the ground. I read it as I read a sky, and walked to the dark.",
    chronomancer: "She stands very still, with a moon behind her head that shows the shape of what she will do before she does it, which is a courtesy I appreciated. What is bright on it is cut on the floor. I will have read it right the first time, I think. I did not.",
    tidecaller: "A tall, silver, silent woman, and behind her head a small moon turning on its own tide. Whatever is lit on it, she cuts on the floor, and the room is round and wide as a bay. I learned to watch the moon first. It rules the water, and it rules her.",
    lanternkeeper: "A still lady in a robe lit down one side, and a thin moon behind her head, like a lamp hung low. What is lit on the crown is cut on the floor. I looked for the dark half of the room and set my lamp there, and was surprised how far away it was.",
    kenseier: "A tall swordswoman with a crescent blade as long as herself, and a small moon behind her head that shows her stroke before she makes it. What is bright on it is cut on the floor. I read the crown as I read a stance, and stood where the stroke was not.",
    houndmaster: "Tall pale lady at the end of the road, silver hair to her boots, blade like a new moon. A little moon behind her head, and whatever's bright on it gets cut on the floor. Rust and Ash sat down in the dark half without being told. Clever lads.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats her
  win: {
    bastion: "The Regent went down on one knee and the crown went dark behind her head. Nothing was left to read. The road ends at this door, and every one of us walked up it on his own boots. I stood at the threshold last.",
    gloomsworn: "She fell, and the little moon behind her head went out like the last lamp on a watch. The road ends here, and I found I wanted it to go on a while. Not for me. Somebody should keep a door like that.",
    thresher: "Down she went at last, dear, quite gracefully, and the crown behind her head faded to a dark little coin. The silver road ends at her door. It was a lovely walk, and everyone arrived. I hummed all the way back down it.",
    bloodreaver: "She fell, the crown went dark, and I stood in the dust counting scars, the whole long road of them, from the frozen lake to this door! I would walk it again. I would even let her cut me twice.",
    runeguard: "The Pale Regent is fallen and the crown is dark. The margin holds every cut I read ahead of it, and the ones I did not. The road ends at her door, and the last line of the entry is the plainest of all: we arrived.",
    gorgemaw: "A long table, a long road, a very fine house at the end of it. The last course went down, and the little moon behind her head went out like a candle on a cake. I would have stayed for the washing-up.",
    oathbroker: "The Regent is discharged, and the crown behind her has gone out. The road is concluded at her door, and every term set along it was met. I have drafted nothing for the next house. For once I arrive with no instrument in hand.",
    ironpalm: "The crown went dark as she knelt. The road ended at the door, and the door was only a door. We stood on the pale floor under the black sky and did not speak. It was a good quiet.",
    ranger: "Down. Crown dark. End of the road, and it's a door with nothing behind it. Four of us walked up. Four stood there. I looked back down the way we'd come, once, and the whole trail was clear.",
    shadowblade: "She sank, and the moon behind her head went black, and for once nothing in the whole round room was bright enough to cast a shadow. I stood in the dark half of the world and found it was mine. The road ends here. I bowed to her.",
    pyromancer: "She fell, the crown went out, and the floor under my boots was only dust under a black sky, with the world hanging over it like a lantern! I have never had a better stage. The road ends here, and the audience was the sky.",
    thermist: "The crown went dark and the room went still. Neither hot nor cold. I stood on the pale floor and the whole long road came back at once, lake and hall and span and door. Balanced. I sat down in the dust.",
    driftblade: "The crown went out as she went down and I was already running, the whole way round the wide floor with nothing left to cut, and the road behind me was one long thread and the door ahead was open.",
    alembist: "Observed: one regent, felled; one crown, dark; every flask whole, and a fresh tin of silver dust for the shelf. The road is walked to its end. Conclusion: the dark half was the right half. It always was.",
    threadweaver: "She is down and the moon has gone off her head like a snuffed lamp. Four of us at the end of a long silver road, and no stitch dropped. I counted twice. Come in, all of you. The house is ours now.",
    marionettist: "The final curtain: our leading lady knelt, the moon behind her went dark, and the great round stage held nothing but the company. (The Doll took her bow in the dark half of the floor, naturally.) The road ends at the door. Applause.",
    cleric: "She knelt, poor lady, and the little moon went out behind her. I counted my friends by the pale light of the sky and every one of them was whole. I said a prayer for the house. The road ends here, and we walked it together.",
    grovekeeper: "She went down, and the little moon behind her head set like a real one, slowly, and the grey field lay quiet under the stars. A long road, and all of my lot walked it to the end. I sat on the cold ground and let it be quiet.",
    chronomancer: "She falls, and the crown goes dark, and I will have walked this road a hundred times by now, though it was only once. Nothing needed undoing at the end. I stayed a little while to be sure it was the end.",
    tidecaller: "The moon behind her went out, and the floor lay still as a bay at slack water, with the whole sky in it. The road ended at her door. I sang a low note for her, as for a tide that has finished going out.",
    lanternkeeper: "The crown went dark, and the room was lit by the sky alone. Four of us on a long road, four lamps, and all four still burning at the door. I set a small lantern on the sill and left it there for whoever comes next.",
    kenseier: "The Regent is down, and the crown is dark. She kept honest time, all of it, from first cut to last; I bowed to her. The road ends at her door. I sheathed the blade, and it was a courtesy to both of us.",
    houndmaster: "Pale lady's down, and the moon's off her head. Rust and Ash came up out of the dust wagging, grey to the knees. End of a long road. Door's open. Home by supper, I'd say.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Moonrise': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She raised her hands and a light blast crossed the room to every one of us. It was small, and there was nothing to raise a shield against. After it the crown woke, and I learned to look at it before I looked at her blade.",
      gloomsworn: "A soft blast came over all of us as she began, hardly worth the name. Then the little moon behind her head lit one edge. I took that for the lesson of the night: the lit part is what she means to cut, and the dark is where a man stands.",
      thresher: "A little blast of light to start, dear, quite polite, and it reached every one of us. Then the crown behind her head lit up in a shape, and that shape is the part of the floor she will cut. Watch the crown first.",
      bloodreaver: "A bright thump reached every one of us as the act began, and it tickled! Then her crown lit up in some shape, and the shape is a promise: whatever shines up there is carved into the floor a moment later.",
      runeguard: "The act opens with a light blast that reaches all, and the entry is brief: be whole when it lands. What follows is the true lesson. The crown takes a shape, and the lit part of it is the part of the round floor that will be struck.",
      gorgemaw: "A light starter, served to every chair at once and quickly gone. Then my hostess's crown took on a bright shape like a bite out of a plate, and that bright shape is what she carves into the floor. I ate from the dark portion.",
      oathbroker: "A modest levy in light fell upon all of us at the opening, requiring only that we be solvent. The crown then displays the next claim: its lit portion is the portion of the floor to be struck, and the dark portion is exempt.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke softly first, a light blast to everyone. No answer to give but to be whole. Then the crown lit in a shape, and I learned to read that shape before the floor spoke it.",
      ranger: "Light blast to open the act, reached everyone, small. Then the crown lit in a shape. That shape is the part of the floor she cuts. Dark part is cover. Mended first, looked up second.",
      shadowblade: "A gentle little blast to everyone, as an introduction, and then the crown behind her head lit in some shape. The lit part is where she is looking, so I went and stood in the part she was not. It is a good habit with hostesses.",
      pyromancer: "A light blast went through all of us as she began, hardly a tickle, and then her crown lit in a shape, a very bright one, and the same bright shape came down on the floor later! I took the dark half of the room, which suited me less.",
      thermist: "Light blast. Everyone. Small, over fast, and I went hot and then cool. Then the crown lit in a shape, and the same shape lands on the floor. Lit means struck. Dark means cool, and I went and stood in the cool.",
      driftblade: "A soft blast to open, over everyone before I had finished turning round, and then the crown behind her head lit up in a shape, and I learned to run by that shape, to the dark part, before the floor ever showed red.",
      alembist: "Observed: a light blast to all of us at the start, mild, treatable with an ordinary dose. Then the crown lights in a shape, and that is the real reading: the lit part is the part of the floor to be struck. Plan the dose and the feet accordingly.",
      threadweaver: "A light blast ran along every thread at once as she began, hardly a tug. Then her crown lit in a shape, and I saw the same shape would be cut in the floor. I brought my stitches to the dark half before the cutting began.",
      marionettist: "Overture: a light blast to the whole company, a polite one. Then our leading lady's crown lit in a shape, and the shape is the cue: whatever is lit up there is the part of the stage that is cut. The company took the dark. (The Doll stood well in the wings.)",
      cleric: "A light blast reached every one of us as the act began. I mended it at once, and it needed little. Then her crown lit in a shape, and I asked everyone to find the dark part of the room, for the bright part would be cut.",
      grovekeeper: "A light blast, like a first gust before the weather, over every one of my lot. Then the crown lit, a shape in it, and the same shape came down on the floor. Bright up there means struck down here. I stood where it was dark.",
      chronomancer: "The blast at the start is light and it comes the moment her hands rise. After it the crown lights in a shape, a good while before the floor does, and I will have moved on the crown, not the floor, and so did the others.",
      tidecaller: "A small swell of light came across the room and washed over all of us. Then the moon behind her head grew a bright edge in some shape, and that edge is where she cuts the floor. The dark part stays dry.",
      lanternkeeper: "A light blast crossed the room at the start, one little gust through every lamp. Then the crown lit in a shape. The lit part of it is the part of the floor she will cut, so I went to the dark part and brought the others.",
      kenseier: "A light blow at the opening, to everyone, with no beat to meet. Then the crown shows a shape, and the shape is the stroke: the bright part is the part of the floor it lands on. I stood where the stroke was not.",
      houndmaster: "Light blast over the lot of us at the start, dogs too, and nothing to it. Then the crown behind her head lit in a shape. Bright up there is cut down here. Whistled the dogs into the dark part and kept them there.",
    } },
    'Crescent': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown showed a thin bright rim on one edge, and the floor took the same rim a moment later. I kept to the wide dark side, line behind me, and the cut landed at the wall. Footing was good all through.",
      gloomsworn: "A thin bright edge on the crown, the rest of it dark, and the dark is home ground for me. We stood in the wide part, middle and all, while the bright rim was cut at the far wall.",
      thresher: "Her little moon wore a thin bright smile, dear, and the floor was cut in the same smile, its tips curling round the sides. So don't stand under the smile. The whole middle is quite safe, and I took it.",
      bloodreaver: "A thin bright rim on the crown, so a thin wound on the floor, hardly worth the trip! I still stayed out of it. The tips curl round the sides, so I stood in the middle with the others and let the rim carve empty dust.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a crown lit only along one edge. The floor is cut in that same crescent, turned the same way, and the whole middle of the room lies in the dark of it. The tips curl round the sides, so I stood well inside them.",
      gorgemaw: "A sliver of crust, lit on one side of the crown, and the same sliver carved from the rim of the floor. Everything else was the soft middle, and that was where my seat was. I kept off the tips, which curl a good way round the plate.",
      oathbroker: "The claim is confined to a narrow crescent along one edge, as the crown declares in advance, and its tips curve round to either side. The remainder of the floor, the middle included, is exempt. I withdrew to the middle with my co-signatories.",
      ironpalm: "A thin bright edge on the crown. The floor spoke it back at the same edge. The wide dark was quiet, so I stood there, away from the curl of the tips, and let the thin word land on nothing.",
      ranger: "Crown lit along one edge only. Floor gets cut along the same edge, tips curling round to the sides. Everything else is cover, middle included. Stepped in from the rim before the red showed and stayed there.",
      shadowblade: "The bright part of the crown was only a thin rim at one edge, which is the whole of what she meant, and the rest of the room she had politely left in shadow. I stood in the shadow, in the middle, and watched the rim go off at the wall.",
      pyromancer: "A thin curve of white fire on her crown, tiny and brilliant, and the same curve lit red across the far edge of the floor! The middle was dark and a great deal safer. I stood in it and was sullenly grateful.",
      thermist: "Thin bright rim on the crown. The rest cold, dark, quiet. The cut came down on the rim of the floor and I was already inside it, in the cool, away from the tips. A small blow, if you are not under it.",
      driftblade: "A thin bright edge on the crown and the floor was cut the same, and I ran in off the rim and straight to the middle, wide of the tips where they curl round, and the whole bright edge landed behind me on bare dust.",
      alembist: "Reading: the crown lit on one thin edge only. Dose: stay in the unlit remainder, which includes the middle, and keep clear of the tips, which curl round to the sides. A small cut, easily avoided and easily mended. Stand where it is dark.",
      threadweaver: "A thin bright seam along one edge of the crown, and the same seam cut along the rim of the floor. I drew my stitches in off the edge to the dark middle, clear of the tips that curl round. Nothing frayed.",
      marionettist: "A single sliver of light along one edge of the crown: a spotlight, and only the edge of the stage is lit by it. The company stood centre stage in the dark. (Keep clear of the tips, which wrap round into the wings.)",
      cleric: "Her crown shone only along one thin edge, and I asked everyone to step in toward the middle, away from that edge and the tips that curl round it. The red was drawn on the rim a little later. Nobody was hurt, and I was glad.",
      grovekeeper: "A thin new moon on her crown, lit along one edge, and the same thin frost on the floor's rim. The middle was bare ground and dark. My lot stood in it, wide of the tips, and the frost fell on the edge of the field.",
      chronomancer: "The crown lit along one edge a good while before the floor did, so I stepped off the rim on the crown and not on the paint. The middle was dark, and so was nearly everything else. We were there already when the red came.",
      tidecaller: "The moon behind her wore a thin bright rim, as on the first night after the dark, and the floor took the same rim at the shore of the room. The middle was dry and dark. We stood in it, off the horns, and the cut broke on the edge.",
      lanternkeeper: "Only one thin edge of the crown was lit. I set my lamp in the dark middle, well inside the curling tips, and the others gathered at it. The cut fell on the rim of the floor, where nobody was standing.",
      kenseier: "A thin bright edge on the crown: a stroke that will land along the rim, tips curled round. The middle is outside the stroke. We stood there, a pace inside the tips, and the cut came down on empty dust.",
      houndmaster: "Thin bright rim on the crown, and the floor got cut the same, round the edge like a crust off a loaf. Middle's dark. Dogs and I stood in it, well in from the tips. Easy, once the crown's read.",
    } },
    'Half Moon': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown showed one bright half, and the floor was cut in half along a line through the very middle. The middle was no ground to hold. I took the dark half, a good pace back from the line, and the rest of us did the same.",
      gloomsworn: "The line ran straight through the middle of the room, and the middle was no place to stand. The crown was half bright, half dark. I took the dark half, well back from the line, and let the other half be cut.",
      thresher: "One half of her little moon is bright and the other dark, dear, and the floor is cut in two along a line through the very middle. So pick the dark half and step well in from the line. A tidy room, divided evenly.",
      bloodreaver: "She cut the room in half like a loaf, a straight line right through the middle, and the bright half took the blow! I like a middle. This was not the night for one. I stood in the dark half, a long stride back from the edge.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a crown lit across exactly half. The floor is cut in the same half, and the dividing line passes through the centre, so the centre is not safe. The safe half is the dark one, and I stood a clear pace inside it.",
      gorgemaw: "She halved the table, bright side to the knife, dark side left on the plate. The cut runs straight through the centrepiece, so the middle is no refuge. I moved to the dark half and sat well back from the edge of the cloth.",
      oathbroker: "One half of the floor is charged, the lit half, as the crown states, and the line of division runs through the exact centre. The centre is therefore no exemption. I stood within the dark half with a margin to spare.",
      ironpalm: "The crown was half bright. The floor was cut along the line through the middle, and the blow fell on the lit side. I stood in the dark half, away from the line, and left her feet if they were lit. I came back after.",
      ranger: "Half the crown lit. Floor splits along a straight line through the centre, bright half cut. Middle's not cover, it's the edge. Moved out into the dark half, clear of the line. Her feet were in the lit half, so I left her and came back.",
      shadowblade: "Half of the crown was bright, so half of the room was hers and the other half was mine. The line between them ran straight through the centre, which is a poor place to stand. I stood well inside my own half, in the dark.",
      pyromancer: "Half her crown blazed, and the floor was cut in half along a line through the very middle! The middle is a terrible seat. I took the dark side, a stride back from the line, and finished my spell there.",
      thermist: "One half of the crown lit. A straight line through the middle of the room, the bright half struck. Not the middle, then, which I wanted. I went to the dark half and stood a good way back from the line, steady.",
      driftblade: "She cut the room in two along a line through the middle, bright half struck, and the dark half was a long run if I had been standing wrong, but I was already crossing it, well back from the line, and the bright half landed behind me.",
      alembist: "Reading: the crown lit across exactly half. Result: the floor is cut in half along a straight line through the centre. Dose: the dark half only, and not on the line, since the middle is the edge. The middle is where everyone wants to stand, so say so.",
      threadweaver: "She cut the room in half along a straight seam through the middle, and the bright half took the cut. I drew my stitches into the dark half, well back from the seam, since the middle is the seam. Nothing was left on the line.",
      marionettist: "The crown lit across exactly half, and the stage was cut along a line through the very middle of it, bright half struck. Centre stage, for once, is the worst place on it. The company stood in the dark half, well back from the line.",
      cleric: "Half of her crown was bright and half dark, and the floor was divided along a line through the centre. I asked everyone to leave the middle, since the line runs through it, and stand well inside the dark half. Nobody lingered.",
      grovekeeper: "Half the field in light and half in shadow, the line between them straight through the middle, like a hedge down a pasture. The lit half was struck. My lot stood in the dark half, well off the hedge.",
      chronomancer: "The crown is half lit, and the line of the cut runs through the middle of the room, so I will have left the middle already. We stood in the dark half, back from the line, and nobody was on the wrong side of it.",
      tidecaller: "One half of the moon bright, and the room divided along a line through the middle like a bay cut by a causeway. The bright half was struck. We waited in the dark half, well back from the line, where the water stayed still.",
      lanternkeeper: "Half the crown was lit, so I lit nothing there. The cut ran along a line through the middle of the floor, bright half struck, and the middle is the line. I hung the lamp well inside the dark half and the others came to it.",
      kenseier: "The crown shows a half, and the stroke is a straight line through the middle of the room: the bright half is struck. The centre is the edge of the stroke, not its shelter. We took the dark half, a full pace back from the line.",
      houndmaster: "Half the crown's bright and the floor's cut straight across through the middle, like a hurdle across a field. Middle's the line, no good standing there. Dogs and I took the dark half, well back, and let the bright half have it.",
    } },
    'Gibbous': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown was nearly all bright, with one thin dark edge. The floor took the same, all but a sliver on the far side. A line cannot hold in a sliver, so I called the move early and we went to the far edge together. Nobody waited for the red.",
      gloomsworn: "All but a sliver of the crown was bright, and the floor was cut the same. The only dark was the thin edge on the far side, a long walk from where we stood. I started walking when the crown showed, not when the red did.",
      thresher: "The crown is nearly all bright, dear, with one thin dark rim, and the floor follows. So don't stand about admiring the shine. Walk to that rim the moment you see it, and tuck in against the edge. It's a long way.",
      bloodreaver: "Almost the whole crown shone and the whole floor with it, all but a thin dark rim far across the room! I ran for that rim before the red had even finished showing, and I loved it. A hard, narrow hiding place.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a crown bright all over but for one thin dark edge. The floor is cut to match, leaving only a sliver on the side opposite the bright one. The walk is long and the red comes late, so the crown is the signal to go.",
      gorgemaw: "The whole plate was served but for one thin strip of rim, on the side opposite the bright part of the crown. I made for it as soon as the crown showed. A hungry man walks quickly to the one dish left.",
      oathbroker: "The crown declares almost the entire floor encumbered, excepting a narrow strip at the far edge, opposite the lit side. The floor shows the claim late, and the distance to the exempt strip is long. I moved on the crown's notice, and not on the floor's.",
      ironpalm: "A crown bright but for one thin dark edge. The floor was cut the same, leaving only that edge. I did not wait for the floor to say it. I walked to the dark edge while the crown was still speaking, and stood there.",
      ranger: "Crown almost all lit, one dark rim. Floor cut to match, sliver of cover on the far side from the bright edge. Long way across. Moved on the crown, not the paint, and leaned in against the rim.",
      shadowblade: "All but a thin edge of the crown was bright, which is a greedy way to cut a room. The only shadow left was the sliver on the far side. I took it at once, before the red had the manners to appear, and stayed pressed against the rim.",
      pyromancer: "Nearly all of her crown blazed, with one thin dark rim, and the floor was cut to match, which left me one narrow strip to stand on! I ran for it the moment the crown showed. I resent running. I resent the alternative more.",
      thermist: "Crown nearly all bright, one thin dark rim. Everything else struck. Far side, narrow, a long run. Go when the crown shows, not when the red does. I ran hot all the way across, and cooled in the sliver, jammed against the edge.",
      driftblade: "The crown was bright all over but one thin edge and I was running before the red showed, straight across the floor to the far rim, the only dark in the whole round room, and I stood there for once and was glad of the wall behind me.",
      alembist: "Reading: the crown lit all over but a thin dark edge. Result: the floor is struck all but a sliver on the side opposite the bright edge. Dose: walk early, on the crown's notice, since the red comes late and the walk is long. Safe quantity: very small.",
      threadweaver: "The crown was bright but for one thin edge, and the floor was cut all but that sliver, on the far side. A long way to carry four stitches. We went the moment the crown showed, and did not wait for the red to say so.",
      marionettist: "Nearly all of the crown lit, one thin dark edge: the whole stage in the light but a strip in the far wings. The company made its exit to that strip the instant the crown showed, and stood close together at the very edge. (A long walk. Do not be late.)",
      cleric: "Her crown was bright but for one thin edge, and I knew we would have to walk far. I called everyone toward that edge as soon as the crown showed, not when the red did, and mended whoever had been slow.",
      grovekeeper: "All the field in the sun but one thin strip of shade at the far fence. The crown showed it first, long before the floor, so my lot started walking at once and were against the fence before the red came.",
      chronomancer: "The crown was bright but for a sliver, and the floor is cut late, so I will have started for the sliver when the crown lit, and the others came along. A late look is a long run, and a long run is a wound.",
      tidecaller: "The moon behind her was full but for a thin dark edge, and the floor took the same, leaving one narrow shallows at the far shore. It is a long way across, so we started when the crown showed, and not when the red rose.",
      lanternkeeper: "The whole crown was lit but one thin edge, so I carried the lamp to that edge as soon as I saw it, and the others followed the light. By the time the red showed, we were against the rim. The walk is long. Start early.",
      kenseier: "A stroke that takes almost the whole room and leaves a sliver at the far edge. The crown shows it first and the floor later, and the distance is long. I moved on the crown, and arrived before the floor had finished showing the stroke.",
      houndmaster: "Whole crown bright but a thin dark rim, and the floor's cut the same, so there's one strip at the far wall to stand in. Long way over. Whistled the dogs and went when the crown lit, not when the red showed. Wall at my back.",
    } },
    'Full Moon': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown went full, and there was no dark part left on it. Nothing was painted on the floor, because the whole floor was struck. I planted my feet, kept near the healer, and took it already mended. It cost every one of us the same.",
      gloomsworn: "A full crown, and not a sliver of dark in it. No floor was painted. There was nowhere to hide, which is a thing I have been told about the grave. We stood close and were mended before it, and mended after.",
      thresher: "When the crown is round and bright all over, dear, there is no dark side to step into and nothing is painted on the floor, because the whole floor is lit. So be mended before it lands, and mended again after. It is a big, bright bell.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole crown shone and the whole floor with it, no painting, no dark corner! It hurt in every inch of me and I could not hit it back. I took it standing, already topped up, near the healer.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a full crown. Nothing is painted, because the whole floor is struck, and no dark remains to retire to. The entry is only this: be whole before it lands, stay near the healer, and be mended after.",
      gorgemaw: "A full plate, served to every chair at once, no portion spared and nothing painted on the cloth to warn us. I stood beside the healer and was filled up beforehand. A heavy dish, thin on subtlety.",
      oathbroker: "A full crown levies the entire floor, without exception, and nothing is painted because nothing is exempt. The remedy is solvency: every party mended beforehand, near the healer, and mended again after. It is the largest single charge of the evening.",
      ironpalm: "The crown was full. No dark edge, no painted floor, nothing to step away from. I stood near the healer and took it whole, mended beforehand. It was a long, bright word, and it ended.",
      ranger: "Crown lit all the way round. No floor paint, no cover, no lane. Whole room's struck. Stayed put near the healer, topped off beforehand. Heaviest thing she throws that can't be dodged.",
      shadowblade: "A full crown leaves no shadow anywhere, and that is the only thing I could say against it. Nothing was painted, nothing was avoidable. I stood beside the healer, mended in advance, and took it with my best grace.",
      pyromancer: "The whole crown blazed, no dark rim anywhere, and nothing was painted on the floor, because there was nowhere left to paint! I stood close to the healer, let her mend me first, and took it. It lit me up from the inside.",
      thermist: "Full crown: no dark, no paint, no cool corner. Everyone struck, the whole floor. Topped off beforehand, stayed near the healer, took it. Ran hot, then steadied. It is the heaviest blast she has that cannot be sidestepped.",
      driftblade: "A full crown and nowhere to run to, nothing painted because the whole floor was struck, which is the cruellest cut of all for someone who cannot stand still, so I ran in close to the healer and took it already mended.",
      alembist: "Reading: crown full, no dark portion, nothing painted. Result: the whole floor struck, no dodging. Dose: everyone brought up beforehand, close to me, and again after. Warning: it is the heaviest hit she throws that cannot be avoided, so do not spend the shelf early.",
      threadweaver: "A full crown, and nothing painted, because every inch of the floor was struck. I drew my stitches in close and mended them to the brim before it landed, then again after. It was the heaviest blast that nobody could step out of.",
      marionettist: "The crown lit all the way round: no dark wings, no painted marks, the whole stage under the lights. Nowhere to exit to. The company gathered near the healer, mended in advance, and took the note together. (A heavy one.)",
      cleric: "Her crown went full, and the whole floor with it, nothing painted and no dark corner left. I laid the light on every one of my friends before it landed, and mended again after. It is the heaviest thing no one can step out of.",
      grovekeeper: "A full moon, and the whole field under it, no shade, no painted ground. I'd planted on every one of my lot before, and they stood close. It broke over us all alike, and we mended after.",
      chronomancer: "The crown full means the floor full, nothing painted, nowhere dark. We gathered, I had everyone mended already, and mended again after the blast. It is the heaviest hit that cannot be dodged. I will have been early for it; I was.",
      tidecaller: "The moon behind her went full and the whole floor lay under it, no shallows and nothing painted. I raised the water high before it came, kept us close, and eased it afterward. It broke over all of us alike.",
      lanternkeeper: "The crown was lit all the way round, so there was no dark half to carry the lamp to, and nothing was painted. I lit all four of us before it landed, close together, and topped the wicks up after. It is the heaviest one.",
      kenseier: "A full crown is a stroke with no edge to turn aside. Nothing is painted, nowhere is dark. We stood close by the healer, mended beforehand, and outlasted it. It is the heaviest blow of the night that cannot be met.",
      houndmaster: "Whole crown lit and no paint, no dark patch. Nothing to get behind. Whistled the dogs in tight beside the healer, got mended before it landed, and mended again. Heaviest thing she throws you can't dodge.",
    } },
    'Earthshine': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She cut at me, and a lighter wedge came a moment behind the first. I held the guard through both and the others stayed out of the wedge. From the saddle she rode my own steps, so I stood still before it and the wake was short.",
      gloomsworn: "A wedge from her to me, long and wide, and a lighter one behind it a breath later. I took both whole and kept the dark drawn in between. From the saddle she rides the road I have walked, and the others kept out of my wake.",
      thresher: "Her cut at our shield comes twice, dear: a big wedge, then a paler one just after. So the shield keeps the guard up through the pair, and everyone else stays out of the wedge. On the hound she rides the shield's footprints, so mind where he has walked.",
      bloodreaver: "She cut at me, a huge pale wedge, and then cut again, softer! Both mine. I kept my guard up between and took them gladly. In the saddle she rode the line I had walked, so I kept my feet quiet and the rest kept off my wake.",
      runeguard: "The sign is her blade lowering toward the shield. The cut is a wedge, and a second, paler wedge follows it a moment later; the ward must stand through both. In the saddle she rides the line the shield has walked, so the party keeps off that line.",
      gorgemaw: "A great slice off the joint for me, and a thinner second slice right behind it. I took both and did not set the knife down between. Nobody else sat in the wedge. From the saddle she carves along my footprints, so keep off my trail.",
      oathbroker: "Two instalments, the second the lesser: a wedge at me, and a paler wedge a moment behind it. I covered both with one defence and my co-signatories kept out of the line. From the saddle she rides the course I have walked, and that course is struck.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke to me with a wedge, and spoke again more quietly behind it. I answered the first and did not drop my arms for the second. In the saddle she rides my footprints, so I walked little, and the others kept off the line.",
      ranger: "Wedge from her to the shield, then a paler one right behind it. Same line both times, nobody else on it. In the saddle she rides the shield's tracks, so the rest of us stayed clear of where he'd walked. Healer mended after the second.",
      shadowblade: "She cut at our shield, and then cut at him again, paler, a moment after, which is an encore. I stayed out of the wedge. From the saddle she rides his footprints, which would be rude if it were not so easy to avoid. I kept off his road.",
      pyromancer: "A huge wedge at the shield and then a paler one just after, like an echo that has lost its nerve! I stood clear of it. From the saddle she rides along wherever the shield has walked, so I never stood in his footprints.",
      thermist: "Wedge at the shield, then a paler one behind it. Stay out of the line. The shield's guard held through both. From the saddle she rides his recent footprints; keep off his trail. I ran cold watching, and was not in it.",
      driftblade: "She cut at the shield and cut again right behind it, paler, and I ran wide of the whole wedge, and from the saddle she rode the shield's own footprints, so I ran wide of those too, and never crossed the trail where he had walked.",
      alembist: "Cause: two cuts at the shield, a wedge and a paler wedge a moment after. Cure: guard held through both, the strong dose after the second, everyone off the line. In the saddle she rides the shield's trail, so nobody stands where he has walked.",
      threadweaver: "Two cuts for our shield, a wedge and a paler one stitched on behind it. I kept the others out of the line and mended after the second, not between. From the saddle she follows the thread he has walked, so we kept clear of it.",
      marionettist: "A duet for our knight: the leading lady's wedge, and her paler reprise a moment after. The company stayed out of the line, and the knight kept the guard up through both. (From the saddle she follows his blocking exactly. Keep off his footprints.)",
      cleric: "Her cut came at our knight twice, one wedge and then a paler one right behind it. I laid the light on him before the first and mended after the second. From the saddle she rides the line he has walked, so I asked the rest to keep off it.",
      grovekeeper: "Two cuts at my big fellow, a wedge and a paler one after, like a thaw following a frost. I'd planted on him before the first and tended after the second. From the saddle she rides the furrow he has walked, and my lot stayed off it.",
      chronomancer: "She cuts at the shield twice, the second paler and just behind, and the second is the one that catches the careless. I mended after it, not between. In the saddle she rides the shield's steps, so we kept off his trail.",
      tidecaller: "A swell at our shield and a paler swell behind it. They broke along the same line, and we stayed out of it. I let the second pass before I raised the water. From the saddle she rides where he has walked, so we kept off his wake.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two cuts at our shield, one wedge and a paler one just behind, so I hung the light on him and kept it burning through both. The rest of us stayed out of the line. From the saddle she rides the path he has walked, and we kept off it.",
      kenseier: "Two strokes at the shield: a wedge, and a paler one close behind. I met the first on its landing and held through the second. The others stayed out of the line. In the saddle her stroke follows my own steps, so I kept them few.",
      houndmaster: "Wedge at the shield-man, then a paler wedge right behind. Guard stayed up through both. Dogs and I off the line, flanks only. Once she's on the big lad she rides the road the shield-man's walked, so we kept off his trail.",
    } },
    'Moonwell': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two shafts of light stood far apart, each wanting two of us inside. A shaft with fewer than two hurt the whole line. I called the nearer to myself and one more, and the other pair took the far one.",
      gloomsworn: "Shafts of light, each wanting two to stand in it, set far apart. If one was short, every one of us paid for it. I stood in the nearer with whoever came, and felt, for once, needed by a hole in the floor.",
      thresher: "Two pillars of light, dear, far apart, and each one wants two of us standing in it. Short of two, the whole party pays. So pair up and each pair take the nearest pillar, and don't all pile into one.",
      bloodreaver: "Pillars of light, two of them, far apart, and each one wanted a pair inside! One standing alone was a blast on the whole party. I grabbed the nearest friend and stood in the nearest pillar, and the others took the far one.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a shaft of light on the floor, and each wants two heroes inside it. If fewer stand there the whole party is struck. With two shafts the party splits into two pairs, each to the nearer one, and no hero stands alone.",
      gorgemaw: "Two cauldrons of light, a long way apart, and each needing two guests at it. A cauldron left short spoils the soup for everybody. I took the nearer one with a friend, and the others took the far one.",
      oathbroker: "Each shaft requires two parties, and a shaft with fewer than two defaults against the whole company. With two shafts, the company divides into pairs, each pair to the nearer. I stood in mine with a co-signatory and did not leave it.",
      ironpalm: "Two shafts of light, far apart. Each wanted two. We went in by twos, to the nearer, and stood. A shaft short of two hurts everyone. I stood with a friend in the nearer one until it passed.",
      ranger: "Two light shafts, far apart, each wants two bodies. Short one and the whole party eats it. Split in pairs, nearest shaft each, quick. Don't cross the room to even them up. Picked my shaft early and held it.",
      shadowblade: "Shafts of light, two of them, each demanding company, which is the opposite of my nature. I stood in the nearer with the one other soul it wanted, and the party was spared. Any shaft left lonely hurts all of us.",
      pyromancer: "Two shafts of pure light on the floor, each wanting two of us standing in it, and I was a great deal too bright to stand with someone! A short shaft burns the whole party. I paired with my neighbour and took the nearer one.",
      thermist: "Two shafts, far apart, two heroes each. Short and the whole party burns. Pair off with whoever's nearest and take the nearer shaft, no wandering. I ran hot until the other pair was in, then cooled.",
      driftblade: "Two shafts of light far apart and each wanting two, and I do not like to be asked to stand still, but I picked the nearer shaft and a partner and stayed there, because a shaft short of two hurts everyone, and the other pair were already in the far one.",
      alembist: "Cause: two shafts of light, each needing two heroes, set far apart. Cure: pair up, nearest shaft each, no one alone. A shaft that goes short hurts the whole party, and is a chip a later blast will finish.",
      threadweaver: "Two shafts of light, and each needed a pair of my stitches inside. I tied my pair together and sent them to the nearer, and the others took the far one. A shaft left short pulled every thread, so nobody was left alone in one.",
      marionettist: "Two spotlights on the floor, far apart, and each wants a pair of players in it, no solos. A light left short brings the whole house down. The company paired up and took the nearer. (Mind the entrance. Nobody takes a solo.)",
      cleric: "Two shafts of light, each wanting two of us standing in it, and a shaft left short hurt every one of us. I took the nearer with a friend and asked the other two to take the far one. Nobody went in alone.",
      grovekeeper: "Two shafts of light, like sun through a gap in the cloud, each wanting two of my lot standing in it. If one was short the whole field felt the frost. We went in by twos to the nearer, and I stayed to the end.",
      chronomancer: "Two shafts of light, two to each, and a shaft with only one in it hurts the whole party. I will have paired up before the cast ended. We went in by twos to the nearer, early, and stayed put until they faded.",
      tidecaller: "Two shafts of light stood far apart like channel marks, each wanting two to stand in it. A shaft left short troubled every one of us. We went to the nearer by twos, without crossing the room, and held until they faded.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two shafts, and each wanted two of us. Four lamps, two shafts, two lamps to each. I set mine in the nearer with a friend, and the other pair took the far one. A shaft short of two hurt us all.",
      kenseier: "Two shafts of light, far apart, and each wants two to stand in it. A shaft with fewer is a blow on the whole party, and a blade cannot turn it. We went in by twos to the nearer and stood there until the light was gone.",
      houndmaster: "Two shafts of light, far apart, two of us to a shaft. A short one hurts the lot of us, dogs too. Picked the nearer with a mate and stayed. Didn't run across the field to even it up. Rust and Ash sat outside.",
    } },
    'Moonlets': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the others had a small round mark over their heads, and a moon fell where each stood, and stayed. The rest of us kept off them while they walked. They set the moons down on the dark side of the crown, apart. When the dance ended we stood clear of the circles.",
      gloomsworn: "A small moon fell on two of us and stayed where it fell, to burst later. Those two walked off alone and set theirs down on the dark side, away from each other. I kept well clear of them while they walked. Stay out of another man's moon.",
      thresher: "Two of us wear a little moon over our heads, dear, and set it down where they stand, and it stays. So they wander off alone to the dark side of the crown, a good way from each other, and the rest of us give them room. It bursts when the dance ends.",
      bloodreaver: "A moon fell on two of my friends, a small round ring that followed them about, and then it stayed where they stopped! They took theirs to the dark side of the crown, far from each other, and I kept back. Each burst later, when the dance ended.",
      runeguard: "A small round sign over two heads: the moon follows its bearer, and where the bearer stands when the cast ends it is set down, and stays. The crown shows the turn of the dance to come; the moons belong on its dark side, apart. They burst when the dance ends.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests were handed a little moon each, and wherever they stood when the cast ended, there it sat, on the cloth, waiting. They put them on the dark side of the crown, apart. I kept off both. The moons go off at the end of the dance.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties are marked, and the moon follows each until the cast ends, then is deposited where they stand. The crown names the dance to come, and the deposit belongs on its dark side, apart from the other. The others stay clear of the carriers until then.",
      ironpalm: "Two of us wore a small round sign. The moon followed them, then was left where they stopped. They went to the dark side of the crown, apart, and set it down. The rest of us stayed out of their rings. It spoke again when the dance ended.",
      ranger: "Two marks over two heads, round, tracking each carrier. Moon sets where they stand when the cast ends and stays. Crown shows the dance's turn: set them on the dark side, far apart. Rest of us off the carriers. They burst at the end of the dance.",
      shadowblade: "A moon fell on two of us and followed them about, and stayed where it was set down. I was not chosen, so I kept well away from the carriers. They took theirs to the dark side of the crown, apart, which is where a shadow would have put it anyway.",
      pyromancer: "I was NOT picked to carry one, to my great relief, but two others were, a small bright moon on each head that follows them and is set down where they stop! They took theirs to the dark side of the crown, far apart, and I stood well off.",
      thermist: "Two of us carry a moon. It follows, then drops where they stand and stays. Crown shows the dance to come: put them on the dark side, apart from each other. The rest of us, off the carriers. Then the blast when the dance ends.",
      driftblade: "A moon on two of us and I was not one, so I ran wide of the carriers while they walked their moons out to the dark side of the crown, far from each other, and set them down, and the moons stayed, and burst when the dance ended.",
      alembist: "Cause: a moon falls on two heroes and is set down where they stop. Cure: the carriers walk alone to the dark side of the crown, apart from each other, and the rest stay clear. Note: the moons stay and burst when the dance ends.",
      threadweaver: "Two of my stitches had a small moon each, following them and then dropped where they stood. I cut them loose from the rest and sent them to the dark side of the crown, far from each other. The moons burst when the dance ended.",
      marionettist: "Two players are each handed a prop moon that follows them, and where they stand when the cast ends, there it stays. They carried theirs to the dark side of the crown, apart, and the rest of the company gave them the stage. (Props go off at the end of the dance.)",
      cleric: "A small moon fell on two of my friends and stayed where they stood. I asked them to carry it to the dark side of the crown, away from each other, and the rest of us stayed off them. It burst when the dance ended, and not before.",
      grovekeeper: "Two of my lot were given a moon each, like seed set down in a row, where they stopped. I sent them to the dark side of the crown, far apart, and the rest of us stayed off them. The moons bloomed when the dance ended.",
      chronomancer: "Two heroes carry a small moon each, and it is set down where they stand when the cast ends. The crown shows the turn of the dance it is for, so I will have put them on the dark side already, apart. They burst when the dance ends.",
      tidecaller: "Two of us were each given a small moon that followed like a buoy, and was left where they stopped. They carried them to the dark side of the crown, far from each other. The rest of us stayed off them, until they burst at the dance's end.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two small moons, two carriers. I was not one, so I kept the others clear of them while they walked to the dark side of the crown and set the moons down far from each other. A lamp left in the wrong place goes out. These burst when the dance ends.",
      kenseier: "A moon falls on two of us and is left where each stops. The crown shows the dance it is meant for, and the dark side of that dance is the place to leave them, apart. The rest of us keep clear of the carriers. They burst when the dance ends.",
      houndmaster: "Two of us got a moon each, round mark over the head, followed us, dropped where we stopped. Took mine to the dark side of the crown, far from the other. Dogs and the rest stayed off the carriers. Goes off when the dance ends.",
    } },
    'Waxing Dance': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Four cuts, all on one turn: a crescent, a half, a gibbous, then a full moon. I read the first and knew the rest. The far edge, opposite the bright side, was dark for every cut, so the line walked there at once. Moons lying there burst after the last cut, so we stepped clear.",
      gloomsworn: "A crescent, then a half, then a gibbous, then a full moon, all turned one way. A man who knows the order knows the last cut from the first. The thin dark edge on the far side was never struck, so I went there once and did not leave.",
      thresher: "A crescent, a half, a gibbous and a full moon, dear, all on one turn, each a little quicker than the last. Clever of her to show you the whole dance in its first step. Walk to the far dark rim at once, and stay. The full moon is the healer's bow.",
      bloodreaver: "Four cuts, each bigger than the last, a crescent, a half, a gibbous and then the whole moon! I saw the first and knew every other. The far edge, away from the bright side, never lit once. I stood in it and took only the last blast, with my friends.",
      runeguard: "The sign is the moon's own order: crescent, half, gibbous, full, all on one turn. From the first cut the others are known. The far edge, opposite the bright side, stays dark through every one, so we went there at once and did not move. The last is a blast for the healer.",
      gorgemaw: "A four-course dance, each course larger than the last: a crescent, a half, a gibbous, then the full moon for dessert, and all served from the same side. The far rim of the floor was never touched. I sat there from the first course.",
      oathbroker: "The dance's four cuts follow the moon's order and share one turn: crescent, half, gibbous, full. The far edge, opposite the lit side, is exempt from each, so the party took it at the first cut and did not leave. The last charge is general.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke in four words, each longer than the last: crescent, half, gibbous, full. The same side lit every time. The far edge never heard any of it. I stood there from the first word, and the last, which reached the whole room, I took mended.",
      ranger: "Four cuts, all on one turn, growing: crescent, half, gibbous, full. Read the first and the rest follow. Far edge, opposite the bright side, is clear through all of them. Walked there at the first cut and held. Last one's a blast, so topped off.",
      shadowblade: "Four cuts in the moon's order, all from the same side, so the last was known from the first, which spoils the surprise but helps the survival. The far edge never saw the light. I was there before the second cut and stayed behind the dark.",
      pyromancer: "Four cuts, each bigger than the last, crescent, half, gibbous, full, all lit from the same side, and I knew the whole dance from the first! The far edge stayed dark through all of it. I stood there and cast, and took only the last blast.",
      thermist: "Crescent, half, gibbous, full. Same side lit each time, growing. Read the first and you have all four. The far edge, opposite the bright side, stays cool through every cut. Went there at the first and stayed put. The last blast I took hot, mended.",
      driftblade: "The dance came in four cuts all turned the same way, small then larger then larger then the whole moon, and I read the first and ran for the far edge, opposite the bright side, and stayed there for once through all of it, because it never lit.",
      alembist: "Reading: four cuts on one turn, in the moon's order: crescent, half, gibbous, full. Result: the far edge, opposite the lit side, is dark for all four. Dose: go there at the first cut, stay put. Moons lying there burst after the last, so step out of them.",
      threadweaver: "Four cuts on one turn, each wider than the last. I read the first and knew the whole seam, and my stitches went to the far edge at once. If moons lay there, we left their circles right after the full moon, before they burst.",
      marionettist: "A four-part dance in the moon's own order, each lighting more of the stage from the same side: crescent, half, gibbous, full. The company took the far wing, which never saw a light, from the first cue. The finale lights everything. (Be mended for it.)",
      cleric: "Four cuts, one turn, each wider than the last. I read the first and knew them all, and called everyone to the far edge, opposite the bright side, where it never lit. I mended before the full moon, and we stepped out of any moon's circle after it.",
      grovekeeper: "The cuts came in the moon's own order, all on one turn, like the sun climbing one side of a field. The far edge never got the light, and we stood in it the whole dance. I'd planted for the last blast, and moons left there we walked clear of.",
      chronomancer: "Each cut lands a little sooner than the last, so I will have known the whole dance from the first: crescent, half, gibbous, full, one turn. The far edge, opposite the bright side, never lit. After the final blast I was already out of any moon's circle.",
      tidecaller: "The moon grew through four cuts, always from the same shore. The far edge stayed dry through every one of them, and we stood there from the first cut. I raised the water high for the last, and we waded clear of any moon that burst after it.",
      lanternkeeper: "Four cuts on one turn, each lighting more than the last, until the whole floor. I set my lamp at the far edge, where the lit side never reached, and the others gathered. I topped up our wicks before the last, and moved the lamp out of any moon's circle after.",
      kenseier: "Four strokes in the moon's order, all on one turn, and each lands a little sooner. The first tells the form of the rest. The far edge, opposite the bright side, is never struck. We stood in it from the first, and met the last, a blast, mended.",
      houndmaster: "Four cuts, all from one side, each bigger. Crescent, half, gibbous, full. First one tells you the lot. Far edge, opposite the bright side, never got touched. Walked the dogs there at the first cut and sat. Last one's a blast, so got mended.",
    } },
    'Waning Dance': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The dance began with the full moon, and only after it did the crown shrink to show which way the rest would turn. I stood near the middle for the blast, then went at once to the thin dark edge. After that each cut was smaller, and the edge held for all of them.",
      gloomsworn: "A full moon first, and then the crown dwindled, a gibbous, a half, a crescent, as a lamp does at the end of a watch. The turn was only shown at the first cut after the blast. I went to the thin dark edge then, and stayed, and each cut was kinder.",
      thresher: "The waning one starts with the full moon, dear, so be mended, and then the crown shrinks to a gibbous that shows which way the rest will turn. That is your cue: run to the thin dark rim, and stay. Half and crescent follow, and they are kind.",
      bloodreaver: "It opened with the whole moon, the worst of it first! Then it shrank, gibbous, half, crescent, and I found the far thin edge and stayed in it, and every cut after was a gentler one. I thought of the full blast as the price of admission.",
      runeguard: "The sign here is a full crown that then wanes: the full moon lands first, and the next crown, a gibbous, shows the turn of all that follow. I read it, went to the thin dark edge, and stayed; half and crescent leave still more room.",
      gorgemaw: "The big course came first, the full moon, and then the courses shrank: gibbous, half, crescent. The turn only showed after the blast, so I stayed near the middle for it, then walked to the thin dark rim. The pickings improved with every cut.",
      oathbroker: "The first charge is general, a full moon, and the turn of what follows is disclosed only after it: a gibbous, then a half, then a crescent, each smaller. I remained near the middle for the first, then took the thin exempt strip, and kept it.",
      ironpalm: "It began with the loudest word, the full moon, and then grew quieter: gibbous, half, crescent. The turn was shown only after the loud one. I stood near the middle until then, went to the thin dark edge, and stayed. Each word after was easier.",
      ranger: "Full moon first. Then the crown shrinks to a gibbous and shows the turn. Go to the thin dark rim at once, stay there, half and crescent are looser. Stood near the middle for the blast so no edge was far.",
      shadowblade: "It opened with the full moon, no shadow anywhere, and only afterwards did the crown narrow to a gibbous and show which side the dark was on. I found it, and stayed in it while the cuts waned around me. Each one left more of the room to hide in.",
      pyromancer: "The full blast came first and the cuts shrank after it, gibbous, half, crescent, and not until the first of them did the crown show which way they would turn! I ran for the thin dark edge at once, and then stood there, delighted, as the dark grew.",
      thermist: "Full moon, then shrinking: gibbous, half, crescent. Turn shown only after the blast. Stand near the middle for it. Then run to the thin dark rim, and stay put: each cut leaves more of the cool. I ran hot for the blast, then cooled.",
      driftblade: "It started with the full moon and nothing to run to, and then the crown narrowed to a gibbous and showed me which way, and I ran to the thin dark edge, and every cut after was smaller and I stayed in the rim, and my legs were glad of it.",
      alembist: "Reading: a full moon first, then shrinking cuts on one turn, gibbous, half, crescent. Catch: the turn is shown only after the blast. Dose: stay near the middle for the first, run to the thin dark rim at the gibbous, and stay. Everything after is easier.",
      threadweaver: "The dance started with the whole floor lit, so I mended before it, and then the crown shrank to a gibbous and showed the turn for all that followed. My stitches went to the thin dark edge at once, and stayed. Half and crescent leave more slack.",
      marionettist: "The finale came first, the full moon, and only then did the cuts shrink for the rest of the act: gibbous, half, crescent. The turn is revealed after the blast. The company stood near the middle for it, then ran for the thin dark wing. (Every cue after is easier.)",
      cleric: "It began with the full moon, so I had everyone mended first, and then the crown shrank to a gibbous and showed which way the rest would turn. I called everyone to the thin dark edge, and we stayed. Half, then crescent, were gentle.",
      grovekeeper: "The big frost came first, the full moon, and then it thawed: gibbous, half, crescent. The turn showed only after the first blast. I stood near the middle for it, then my lot walked to the thin dark edge and stayed. Every cut was gentler.",
      chronomancer: "The full moon comes first, and the turn comes only after it, when the crown shrinks to a gibbous. I stood near the middle so no edge was far, then went to the thin dark rim, and stayed through half and crescent. I will have gone sooner, had I known.",
      tidecaller: "The full tide first, and then it ebbed: gibbous, half, crescent, each smaller. The shore it ebbed toward was only shown after the first blast. I stood near the middle, then went to the thin dry rim and stayed. The water was kinder after.",
      lanternkeeper: "It opened with the whole floor lit, so I lit us all before it, and after it the crown narrowed to a gibbous and showed where the dark was. I carried the lamp to the thin dark rim and we stayed there as the cuts grew smaller.",
      kenseier: "The first stroke is the whole room, and the rest wane: gibbous, half, crescent. The turn is shown only after the first lands. I stood near the middle for it, then took the thin dark edge, and held it as the strokes grew shorter.",
      houndmaster: "Opened with the full moon, nothing to dodge, then shrank to a gibbous that showed which way it was turned. Dogs and I walked to the thin dark rim and sat. Half, then crescent, easier each time. Stood near the middle for the blast so no edge was far.",
    } },
    'New Moon': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She bowed, the crown went dark, and the floor dimmed around our boots. A light blast crossed the room at the end of it. Report: the dark hides nothing. Every danger is painted as bright as ever, and the ground I could see was the ground I had.",
      gloomsworn: "She bowed to us, and the crown went black, and the room dimmed. I was at home in it. The light blast at the end of the bow reached everyone. What she paints in the dark is as plain as ever: red for harm, blue for the place to stand together.",
      thresher: "A deep, slow bow, dear, and the crown goes out and the floor dims, and a small blast of light follows to the whole party. It looks like night, but the paint on the floor is as bright as ever. If it's red, don't stand in it. If it's blue, do.",
      bloodreaver: "She bowed, the crown went black and the whole floor dimmed, and a light thump reached every one of us at the end! I thought the dark would hide things. It hid nothing. Red was as red as ever, and I kept out of it.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a bow, the crown going dark and the floor dimming. A light blast follows at the end of it. Nothing is hidden in the dark: red remains red, blue remains blue, and the paint is as bright as ever. The entry reads: believe the paint, not the dimness.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess bowed and the lamps went down, the crown with them, and a light blast went round the table at the end. A dim room and a bright sauce: every painted danger shone as clearly as before. I stood in what was blue and avoided what was red.",
      oathbroker: "She bows, the crown is extinguished, the floor dims, and a light levy falls on every party at the end. The terms are unchanged by the dark: every danger is painted as brightly as before, red to avoid and blue to share. The dimness is irrelevant. I read the paint.",
      ironpalm: "She bowed. The crown went dark, the floor dimmed, and a light blast reached all of us at the end of the bow. I could not read the crown, but I could read the paint, and it was as bright as it ever was. Red was loud. Blue was kind.",
      ranger: "Bow, then the crown goes black and the floor dims. Light blast at the end of it, everyone. Dark doesn't hide anything: every painted danger is as bright as before. No crown left to read, so read the paint, red out, blue in.",
      shadowblade: "She bowed, and the crown went out, and the room dimmed, which I took personally, as a rival's trespass. A light blast followed. The dark did not hide her paint, which stayed as bright as ever. Red to avoid, blue to share.",
      pyromancer: "She bowed, the crown went black, and the whole floor dimmed like a theatre before the curtain, then a light blast at the end of the bow! But every painted danger stayed as bright as ever. Dark floor, bright paint. I watched the paint and ignored the gloom.",
      thermist: "A bow, then the crown goes dark and the floor dims. Light blast at the end of it. Cold room, bright paint: every danger shows as plainly as before, red to avoid and blue to share. I went still, read the paint, and moved when it said.",
      driftblade: "She bowed and the crown went out and the floor went dim and a light blast crossed the room, and I was ready to run, but there was nothing to run from yet, and the paint, when it came, was as bright as ever, red to avoid and blue to gather in.",
      alembist: "Reading: a bow, the crown dark, the floor dimmed, and a light blast at the end. Result: nothing hidden. Every painted danger is as bright as before. Dose: mend the blast, then read the paint, not the gloom. Red to avoid, blue to gather in.",
      threadweaver: "She bowed, and the crown went dark and the floor dimmed, and a light blast tugged at every thread at once. I mended it quickly. Then I told everyone the paint was as bright as ever and to follow it, red to avoid, blue to gather in.",
      marionettist: "House lights down: she bows, the crown goes dark, the floor dims, and a light blast reaches the whole company at the end of the bow. Every mark is still painted as brightly as before, red to avoid and blue to share. A dim stage, a clear script.",
      cleric: "She bowed, the crown went dark and the floor dimmed, and a light blast reached every one of us at the end. I mended it and told everyone not to be afraid of the dark: every painted danger was as bright as before.",
      grovekeeper: "A bow, and then dusk came down on the field: the crown dark, the floor dim, a light blast over every one of my lot. Dusk hides things, but not this. Every painted danger shone as plainly as before. We read the paint, and stood or stepped as it said.",
      chronomancer: "She bows, and the crown goes dark, and the floor dims, and then the light blast at the end of the bow. It looks as if the lights went out, but the paint is as bright as ever. I will have been ready for it; we read the paint, not the dark.",
      tidecaller: "She bowed, the moon behind her went out, and the floor dimmed like the sea at dusk, and a light swell crossed the room at the end. The dark hid nothing. The paint shone as bright as ever, red to avoid, blue to gather in.",
      lanternkeeper: "She bowed, and the crown went dark, and the floor dimmed, and a light blast crossed the room at the end. It looked as though every lamp had gone out, but the paint shone as brightly as ever, so I lit nothing and read the paint.",
      kenseier: "She bows: the crown goes out, the floor dims, and a light blow reaches all of us at the end. The dark has no effect on the strokes; every painted danger is as bright as before. A teacher who lowers the lamps still has to show the form.",
      houndmaster: "Bow, then the crown goes dark and the floor dims, and a light blast over the lot of us. Dark doesn't hide anything, every bit of paint's as bright as ever. Red out, blue in. Dogs didn't like the dim but stayed close and read it fine.",
    } },
    'Moonless Steps': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A thin silver line ran from her to one of us, a number over his head. It locked, and she dashed along it. The marked one led it away from the rest and stepped aside. The rest of us kept out of the line and stood together in the pool of light.",
      gloomsworn: "A number appeared over one of us and a thin silver line ran to him. When it locked she dashed along it, and a small crescent was cut at the end. The marked one walked clear of us first, then stepped off the line. I stood with the others in the blue light.",
      thresher: "A little number over somebody's head, dear, and a thin silver line from her to them. They walk well away from us, step off the line as it locks, and she dashes through empty floor. The rest of us gather in the pool of light, which shrinks each time.",
      bloodreaver: "A number over one of my friends and a silver line to him, and then she DASHED along it! He drew it away from us, and stepped aside as it locked. A small cut came down at the end of it. The rest of us stood together in the shrinking pool of light.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a number over a head and a thin silver line from her to that hero. The line locks, she dashes along it, and a small crescent falls at its end. The marked hero leads the line away from the party and steps off it; the rest gather in the pool, which shrinks.",
      gorgemaw: "A thin silver skewer ran from my hostess to one guest, marked with a number. The marked guest took it off to a far corner and stepped aside as it locked, and the rest of us huddled in the little pool of light. Each pool is a smaller plate than the last.",
      oathbroker: "A number names one party, and a silver line runs to them. It locks, and she dashes along it, with a small crescent at its end. The named party leads the line clear of the others and steps off as it locks. The rest of us stand in the pool of light, which diminishes.",
      ironpalm: "A number over one of us, a thin silver line to them. It locked, and she ran it. The marked one walked it away from us, then stepped off. A small crescent fell at its end. The rest of us stood close in the pool of light, which grew smaller each time.",
      ranger: "Number over a head, silver line from her to them. Line locks, she dashes it, small crescent lands past the end. Marked one pulls the line off from the party, steps off it at the lock. Rest of us in the pool of light, shrinking each round.",
      shadowblade: "A number landed on someone else, which was a relief, and a silver line ran to them from the dark. When it locked she dashed along it. I kept out of the line and out of the small crescent at its end, and stood in the pool with the rest of the party.",
      pyromancer: "A number over someone else's head, thank goodness, and a thin silver line to them! It locked and she dashed along it, and a small crescent came down at the end. I stood in the pool of light with the rest, well off the line, which shrank every round.",
      thermist: "Number, line, dash: one hero at a time. The marked one runs clear, steps aside on the lock. The crescent lands past the end, so don't stand beyond them. The rest in the pool. It shrinks. Stay in close, stay cool.",
      driftblade: "A number over a head and a silver line from her to it, and when it locked she dashed along it, so the marked one ran wide of all of us first, and sidestepped as it locked, and the rest of us stayed in the pool of light, which got smaller every time.",
      alembist: "Cause: a number on one hero, a silver line to them, a dash along it, a small crescent at its end. Cure: the marked hero leads the line off from the party and steps aside at the lock. Everyone else holds the pool of light, which shrinks each round.",
      threadweaver: "A number over one of my stitches and a silver thread from her to them. When it locked she dashed along it. The marked one drew the thread off from the rest of us and stepped out of it, and the others stayed close in the pool of light.",
      marionettist: "A number over one of the company and a silver line from the star to them: their cue. The line locks, she dashes along it, a small crescent follows. The marked player leads it into the wings and steps off. The rest hold the pool of light, which shrinks with every scene.",
      cleric: "A number appeared over one of my friends, with a silver line to them. When it locked she dashed along it. I asked the marked one to lead it away from us and step aside, and the rest of us stayed together in the pool of light, which shrank each time.",
      grovekeeper: "A number over one of my lot and a thin silver line like a furrow drawn across the field. It locked, and she dashed along it. The marked one led it away from us and stepped out of the row. The rest of us stayed close in the pool of light.",
      chronomancer: "A number over a head, the line locks, the dash comes, and a small crescent lands past its end. I will have known whose number it was before they did. The marked one led it off from us and stepped aside, and the rest held the pool of light, shrinking.",
      tidecaller: "One of us at a time wore a number. The line to them locked, she dashed, and a small crescent fell past the end of it. The marked one waded away from the rest first and stepped aside; we stood close in the shrinking pool of light.",
      lanternkeeper: "A number over one of my friends, a silver line to them. I counted the dashes, one lamp each. The marked one led the line away from the rest of us and stepped out of it as it locked. The others stayed in the pool of light, which burned smaller every time.",
      kenseier: "One dash at a time, each foretold by a line to a numbered hero. The line locks and the dash follows on the next beat, then a small crescent past the end. The marked one draws it clear and steps off. The rest of us stand close in the pool.",
      houndmaster: "She dashed at one of us at a time, number over the head. Marked one drew it off clear of the rest and sidestepped when it locked. Small crescent past the end. Pool of light shrank each time; we shuffled in, dogs and all.",
    } },
    'The Last Light': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One last pool of light, bigger than the others, and this one wanted every one of us inside. I held the near edge and called the line in. When we were all in it, the room went still. That was the time to be mended, because the full moon followed.",
      gloomsworn: "The last of the light was laid down, a wide pool, and it wanted all of us in it, none left out. I stood in it, and counted heads, which is a habit of the watch. Then nothing came. Mend in the quiet. The brightness comes after.",
      thresher: "One more pool of light, dear, and a wide one, and everybody has to be in it, even the shy ones. Walk in together, and then the whole room holds its breath. That is the moment to be mended, because the full moon is coming.",
      bloodreaver: "A big last pool of light and every one of us had to be in it, or it would be laid again! I herded my friends in, and everyone was in, and then the room went dead still. A calm like that is only the breath before the blow.",
      runeguard: "The sign is the widest pool yet, and the condition is that every hero stands in it. If one is missing, it is laid again. When all are within, the room holds still and nothing is painted. Use that stillness to mend: a full moon is next.",
      gorgemaw: "The last pool of light, wide as a banquet table, and every guest was required to be seated at it. One missing and the course was served again. When all of us were in, the room went quiet. I let the healer fill us up, for the main dish follows.",
      oathbroker: "The final instrument requires every party to stand within the pool, without exception. A default is cured by its being laid again. When all stood within it, the room fell still, and nothing was drawn. That interval is for mending. The full moon follows.",
      ironpalm: "A last, wide pool of light. All of us had to stand in it. We went in together and stood close. The room fell silent, with nothing on the floor. In that silence I did not move, and the healer mended us. The full moon was coming.",
      ranger: "Big pool, last one. Everyone in it or it comes round again. Went in with the rest, all four. Then dead quiet, nothing painted. Time to top up. Full moon's next, and it's a heavy one.",
      shadowblade: "One last pool of light, and everyone had to stand in it, which spoils a fine night for solitude. We went in together. Then the room held still, with nothing painted, and I let the healer work. The light comes back hard, after.",
      pyromancer: "The last pool of light, enormous, and every single one of us had to stand in it, even me, even in the glare! We all went in. Then everything went quiet and the floor was bare. I took the quiet to be mended, for the big blast comes after.",
      thermist: "Wide last pool of light. All four in. No one out, or it is laid again. We went in as one, and then the room went still, and the floor was bare. Cool, calm, a breath. Mend now, because the full moon is coming and it will be hot.",
      driftblade: "One last pool of light, a wide one, and everyone had to be in it, and I hate to stand still but I stood, and so did the rest, and the room held its breath with nothing painted on the floor, and I let the healer top me up before the moon came back.",
      alembist: "Cause: the last pool of light, wide, wanting every hero inside. Cure: all four in it, together, at once; a miss is laid again. Then a stillness, with nothing on the floor. Dose: use it to top everyone up. The full moon comes next.",
      threadweaver: "A last, wide pool of light, and every one of my stitches had to stand in it, or it would be laid again. We drew close, all tied in. Then the room fell still and the floor was bare, and I mended each of us to the brim before the moon came back.",
      marionettist: "The last pool of light, wide as a stage, and every player must stand in it or the scene is played again. The company took their marks together. Then a hush, with nothing on the boards. (Use the pause to be mended. The finale follows.)",
      cleric: "A last pool of light, wide and warm, and every one of us had to stand in it. I called everyone in and counted them, and then the room went still. I used the stillness to mend each of my friends before the full moon came.",
      grovekeeper: "The last pool of light, wide as a clearing, and all of my lot had to stand in it. We gathered in one clump. Then the field went quiet, and the floor was bare. I'd planted for the blast that follows, and I used the quiet to tend.",
      chronomancer: "The last pool of light wants every one of us inside it. If it is missed it is laid again, and the time is lost. We went in together, and the room held its breath. I will have mended everyone in the pause. The full moon was next.",
      tidecaller: "The last pool of light lay wide on the floor, and every one of us had to stand in it. We waded in together, and the room went still as slack water, with nothing painted. I let the water rise in that quiet, for the full moon came after.",
      lanternkeeper: "The last pool of light, wide enough for four lamps and four of us. Everyone had to be in it, or it was laid again. We stood close. The room went still, with nothing painted, and I filled every wick I could before the moon came back.",
      kenseier: "The last pool is wide, and the form requires all four inside it. A miss is laid again. When we were all within, the room held still, with no stroke on the floor. That is the rest before the blow. I used it to draw the ash about me, and the healer mended.",
      houndmaster: "Last pool of light, a wide one, whole party in it or it comes round again. Whistled the dogs in and stood the lot of us shoulder to shoulder. Room went quiet, nothing on the floor. Got mended in the quiet. Full moon's next.",
    } },
    "The Hound's Return": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A howl came up the road before anything showed. I knew the sound. Whoever has walked the most in the last moments is the one he hunts, and he lands where their steps end. One of us walked long and away from the rest, and the others stood still. She ended where that walk did.",
      gloomsworn: "His howl came before he did, and I knew it: the hound from the frozen lake. He runs the steps of whoever has walked the most just before. One of us walked a long way off, and stepped aside at the end. The rest of us stood still on our posts, off his road.",
      thresher: "Somebody's howling up the road, dear, and I know that voice. Whoever has walked most lately is the one he hunts, and he runs their steps and lands where they ended. So one of us takes a long walk, away from everyone, and steps aside, and the rest stand still.",
      bloodreaver: "The howl came up the road and I knew the big one at once! Whoever walks the most is who he hunts, so one of us ran a long line, far from the rest, and stepped aside, and the long walk made a small landing. I stood still, wanting to run.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a howl, and the margin already holds the rule: he hunts the hero who has walked the most, runs their steps, and lands where they end. One hero walks a long line apart and steps off at its end; the rest stand still, off the road and clear of where it began.",
      gorgemaw: "A howl from down the road, and my old host from the lake came up it, hungry. He hunts whoever has walked the most just before, along their very footprints. One guest walked a long line away from the table and stepped off the end. The rest of us sat still, off his road.",
      oathbroker: "A howl, and the old terms apply: the Hound pursues the party who has walked the most in the interval, along the steps so walked, and falls where they end. One party walked a long line apart and stepped aside. The rest of us stood perfectly still and clear of the road.",
      ironpalm: "He howled before he was seen, and I knew his voice. He answers whoever walked most, and runs their steps. One of us walked a long way off, and stepped aside at the end. The rest of us stood still, and the road was empty of us.",
      ranger: "Howl up the road. Same hound. Hunts whoever walked the most lately, runs their tracks, lands where they end. So one walks a long line, away from everybody, and steps off the end. The rest stand dead still and off the road. Long walk, small landing.",
      shadowblade: "His howl came before he did, which is how he introduces himself. He hunts whoever has been most active, so I stood perfectly still, which I do beautifully, and let someone else take a long walk away from us and step off the end. The road stayed clear of me.",
      pyromancer: "A howl up the road, and I knew the big hound from the lake! He hunts whoever has walked the most lately, so one of us took a long, dramatic walk away from the rest, and stepped aside. I stood so still I felt like furniture, well off his road.",
      thermist: "Howl, far off. The hound from the lake. He hunts whoever has walked the most, runs their steps, lands at the end. One of us walked a long way off and stepped aside; the rest stood frozen. I held still and ran cold. A long walk is a small landing.",
      driftblade: "His howl came up the road before I could see him, the hound from the lake, and he hunts whoever has run the most lately, and I would have loved it to be me, so I ran a long line away from the rest of them, and stepped aside before the landing.",
      alembist: "Cause: a howl, the hound from the lake, hunting whoever has walked the most. Cure: one hero walks a long line apart and steps aside at its end, small landing. The rest stand still, off the road. Note: a hero who never moved is fallen on, wide.",
      threadweaver: "A howl came up the road, and I knew that dog. He follows whoever has walked the most, along their seam, and lands where it ends. One of my stitches walked a long way off from the rest and stepped aside. The others held perfectly still, off the road.",
      marionettist: "A howl offstage, and the leading hound enters, one we know. He hunts whoever has crossed the stage the most, along their own route, and lands where it ends. One player took a long cross away from the company and stepped aside. The rest stood still, off the route. (The Doll hid.)",
      cleric: "His howl came up the road and I knew him, poor creature, from the lake. He hunts whoever has walked the most. I asked one friend to walk a long way off, away from the rest, and step aside at the end, and begged everyone else to stand still, off his road.",
      grovekeeper: "The old wolf howled up the road, and the whole field went quiet. He hunts whoever has walked the most, along their own furrow. One of my lot ploughed a long straight row away from the rest and stepped aside. The rest of us stood like fenceposts, off the row.",
      chronomancer: "The howl comes first, and then he, and the freshest trail in the dust is the one he runs. I will have walked a long line, away from the rest, and stepped aside before he landed. The others stood still. A long walk makes a small landing.",
      tidecaller: "A howl rolled up the road like a swell with nothing behind it. He hunts whoever has walked the most, and runs their wake. One of us walked a long, slow line apart, and stepped aside at the end. The rest of us stood still as stones, off the road.",
      lanternkeeper: "A howl came up the road before any shape did, and I knew whose it was. He hunts whoever has walked the most lately. One of us walked a long line away from the others, and stepped off its end. Three of us stood still, off the road, and kept our lamps low.",
      kenseier: "His howl is the count before the stroke. He hunts whoever has walked the most in the last moments, runs their steps and lands where they end. One of us walked a long line apart and stepped aside. The rest stood still and off the road. She ended where he landed.",
      houndmaster: "Howl up the road before I saw him, and I knew the big lad from the lake. He hunts whoever's walked the most, runs their prints, drops where they end. One of us walked a long line off, stepped aside. Rest stood still and off the road. Dogs lay down. Stood dead still myself.",
    } },
    "Hunt's Pass": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She left my guard to ride a marked hero's steps, and for those moments there was nothing of her to hold. She came back to where she had stood. Meanwhile the crown showed the shape of the cut she makes on the way, and the line kept to its dark, off the road.",
      gloomsworn: "It was the hound's old rule from the frozen lake, and she rode it: he goes where a trail goes. A mark came over one of us, and the crown lit a shape for the cut she makes on the way. The marked one's long walk ran through the dark of it.",
      thresher: "The little mark landed on me, dear. So I took a lovely long walk through the dark part of the shape the crown had shown, and stepped aside at the end, and her ride and her cut both found nobody.",
      bloodreaver: "The mark was on someone else and I sulked about it. Still, her ride is the road they walked, and her cut is the shape on the crown, and my part was easy: stand in the dark of that shape, and nowhere near the road.",
      runeguard: "Two entries, read in order: the shape on the crown gives the cut, and the mark gives the road. The road follows the marked hero's own steps; the cut is a fixed shape. Set the walk in the dark of the shape, and the rest of us beside it, off the road.",
      gorgemaw: "Two orders at the one table: the carving shape on the crown, and the marked guest's footprints for her ride. I stayed off the road and in the dark portion of the shape, and let the marked one stroll a long way and step aside.",
      oathbroker: "Two instruments in force at once. First, the shape declared on the crown, binding the floor. Second, the marked party's steps, binding the road. I stood within the exempt portion of the first and clear of the second, and the company followed.",
      ironpalm: "Two things at once: a mark, and a shape. I did not choose between them. I read the shape for where to stand and the mark for where not to stand, and I stood still in the dark, off the road.",
      ranger: "Marked hero's line has to sit inside the dark of the shape. Check the crown first, then walk. Mark was on the other fellow, so I stayed off his trail and in the dark. He walked long, so the pounce came small.",
      shadowblade: "The mark landed on me, naturally. I walked a long, quiet line through the dark part of the shape on the crown, stepped aside at the end, and watched her ride my footprints down to nothing.",
      pyromancer: "I was not the marked one, to my enormous annoyance, so my part was to stand in the dark of the crown's shape, off the road, and watch her ride. The pounce at the end shook the floor, but not me.",
      thermist: "Two readings, fast: the crown for the cut, the road for the ride. The marked one walked long, so the landing was small. I stood in the dark of the first and off the second. Both clear, and I breathed.",
      driftblade: "The mark came to me and I ran the longest line I could find through the dark of the cut, flat out, and stepped off the end of it as the hound came down, and a long run makes a small landing, which is all I know about love.",
      alembist: "Cause: she rides the marked hero's trail and cuts a shape as she passes. Cure: the walker keeps the line long and inside the dark of the cut; the party stays in that dark and off the road. Note: a short walk means a wide landing.",
      threadweaver: "My marked stitch ran a long seam, and I made sure the whole seam lay in the dark of the crown's shape, since it had to be sewn before the cut landed. The rest of us stayed off the thread. I mended the pounce's blast after.",
      marionettist: "The star leaves the stage to ride the marked player's route, and returns to her mark afterward. The crown gives the cut in the meantime. The marked player crossed long, inside its dark. The rest of the company stood in the dark and off the route.",
      cleric: "The pounce at the end of her ride shakes the floor and reaches all of us, lightly, so I had everyone mended in advance. The marked one's long walk lay in the dark of the crown's shape, and the rest of us stood there too, off the road.",
      grovekeeper: "A long ride on the hound, and a cut as she passes, like hail on a ploughing day. The marked one ploughed a long furrow inside the dark of the shape, the rest of us stayed off it, and when she landed the ground shook lightly.",
      chronomancer: "I will have read the shape on the crown before the ride began, because the cut is painted early and lands just after she sets off. The marked one's line lay in its dark. We stood in the dark, off the road, and it passed.",
      tidecaller: "A mark rose over one of us and the moon behind her took a shape. She rode their wake on the hound, a long swell down the floor, and cut the shape as she passed. The marked one swam a long line through the dark, and the rest of us kept off the wake.",
      lanternkeeper: "I carried the lamp to the dark of the shape on the crown, and the marked one walked their long line to it. The road she rode was the line they had walked, so none of us stood on it. A small blast came at the end.",
      kenseier: "Two strokes at once: a shape on the crown, and the road the hound runs after the marked one. The shape is a stroke you can step out of. The road follows a trail you leave. I read both, stood in the dark of the first, and stayed off the second.",
      houndmaster: "I know a hound on a line. This one runs the marked man's own trail, and she cuts a shape as she goes by. Marked one walked long, in the dark of the shape. Dogs and I stood in that dark, well off the road.",
    } },
    'The Long Eclipse': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown dimmed, the room held still, and then a red edge crept onto it. From that moment the rule was turned over: what is dark on the crown is what she cuts. A light blast crossed the room. I told the line: stand in the bright part now, and watch the red grow.",
      gloomsworn: "The crown dimmed and the room went quiet, and then a red edge took hold of it, and the rule turned over: the dark part is the part she cuts now. I was used to the dark being safe. A light blast followed. The bright part is where the living stand.",
      thresher: "Oh, dear, the little moon dims, and everything holds its breath, and a red edge slides onto it. The rule turns over! Now it's the dark part of the crown that is cut, so stand in the bright part. A small blast of light follows, and the red keeps creeping. It is a clock.",
      bloodreaver: "The crown dimmed, everything went still, and then red crept over its edge and the rule flipped! Now the dark part of the crown is the part that gets carved. A light thump followed. I like a rule that changes. The red keeps spreading, so the fight is a race.",
      runeguard: "The sign is the dimming of the crown, a stillness, and a red edge. The rule is then turned over: the dark of the crown is what is struck, and the lit part is safe. The floor paint remains true. A light blast follows, and the red will cover the crown in the end.",
      gorgemaw: "The crown dimmed like a lamp at the end of a long dinner, and then a red edge crept onto it. The rule of the table turned over: the dark part of the crown is what gets carved now, and the lit part is safe. A light blast followed. The red is the bill coming.",
      oathbroker: "The crown dims, a stillness follows, and a red edge appears. By this the terms are inverted: the dark portion of the crown is now what is struck, and the lit portion is exempt. A light levy falls on all at its end. The red will cover the crown, and the account closes.",
      ironpalm: "The crown dimmed and the room went silent. A red edge crept on. The rule turned over: the dark of the crown is what she strikes now, and the bright part is where to stand. A light blast reached us. The red spreads, and when it covers the crown, she is finished with us.",
      ranger: "Crown dims, room still, red edge creeps on. Rule flips: now the dark part of the crown is what gets cut, and the lit part is safe. Floor paint still shows it true. Light blast at the end. Red keeps spreading across the crown. That's the clock.",
      shadowblade: "The crown dimmed, and a red edge crept onto it, and the rule turned over, which was tasteless: now the dark of the crown is what she strikes, and the light is where to stand. A light blast crossed us at the end. The red keeps spreading, and when it covers all, so does she.",
      pyromancer: "The crown dimmed and a red edge crept on, and the rule turned inside out! Now the DARK part of the crown is cut and the bright part is safe, the opposite of everything I had learned, and I had to unlearn it at once. A light blast followed. The red keeps spreading.",
      thermist: "Crown dims, a hush, then a red edge. The rule inverts: the dark of the crown is struck, the bright part is safe. The floor paint stays true. A light blast at the end. The red creeps over the crown the whole fight. I ran cold, then hot, then settled.",
      driftblade: "The crown dimmed and went still and then a red edge crept on, and the rule turned over, so the bright part was the safe part and I had to run to the light for once, and a light blast crossed the room, and the red kept creeping, and we had no time to waste.",
      alembist: "Reading: the crown dims, then a red edge. Result: the rule inverts, so the dark part of the crown is struck and the lit part is safe. The paint on the floor is unchanged. A light blast at the end. Note: the red spreads all fight. That is the shelf life.",
      threadweaver: "The crown dimmed, and a red edge crept onto it, and the rule turned over like a garment inside out: the dark part of the crown is cut now, and the bright part is safe. A light blast tugged every thread. The red keeps spreading, and the seams are running out.",
      marionettist: "Act Five: the crown dims, the house goes still, a red edge creeps on, and the rule turns over. Now the dark part of the crown is the part of the stage that is cut, and the bright part is safe. A light blast at the end. The red spreads, and the play runs short.",
      cleric: "The crown dimmed, and a red edge crept on, and the rule turned over: now the dark part of the crown is the part she cuts, and the bright part is where to stand. A light blast reached us. I reminded everyone gently, and then again. The red keeps spreading.",
      grovekeeper: "The crown dimmed like a sun going into cloud, and a red edge crept on, and the rule turned over. The dark part of the crown is what is cut now, and the bright part is where we stand. A light blast came. The red spreads like frost. The season is ending.",
      chronomancer: "The crown dims, and there is a hush, and a red edge, and then the rule is the other way round: the dark of the crown is what is struck. I will have turned over my habits in the hush. A light blast followed. The red spreads all fight. That is the clock.",
      tidecaller: "The moon behind her dimmed, and the room held its breath, and a red edge crept across the crown like blood in water. The rule turned over: the dark part is cut now, and the bright part is safe. A light swell followed. The red is a rising tide, and it does not turn.",
      lanternkeeper: "The crown dimmed, and a red edge crept onto it, and the rule turned over: the dark part of the crown is what is cut now, so the lamp goes where it is bright. A light blast crossed the room. The red keeps spreading, a wick burning down.",
      kenseier: "The crown dims, and a red edge enters. That is the form turned over: the dark part of the crown is the stroke now, and the bright part is the shelter. The paint on the floor is true. A light blow reaches all of us. The red spreads, and it is the count.",
      houndmaster: "Crown went dim, room went still, red crept on at the edge. Rule's turned over: dark part of the crown gets cut now, bright part is safe. Paint on the floor still tells the truth. Light blast came over the lot of us. Red keeps spreading, like a fire along a hedge.",
    } },
    'Umbral Crescent': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown showed a thin bright rim, and I nearly stood in the middle out of habit. The rule is turned over: the dark is struck, so nearly the whole floor was red and only the bright rim was safe. We went to the edge where the crown was lit, and stayed on it.",
      gloomsworn: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and everything else struck. The dark is the danger now, and the living are the thin edge that is lit. We walked to that edge together and stood on it. I have never been so grateful to be on the rim of anything.",
      thresher: "A thin bright smile on the crown, dear, and under the new rule it's the dark that is cut: nearly all the floor, except the little bright rim. So scoot to that rim, all of us, and stand squeezed in it. A tight fit, but a safe one.",
      bloodreaver: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and nearly the whole floor went red, because the dark is what is cut now! The only safe place was that thin bright edge. I ran to it with my friends, squeezed in, and took nothing.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a crown lit along a thin edge, and under the turned rule the dark of it is struck: nearly the whole floor. The thin bright rim, turned the way the crown is turned, is the only safe ground. We went to it at once, and kept close together.",
      gorgemaw: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and the rest of the plate cut. The dark is carved now, so the only unserved portion was the thin bright edge, on the side the crown was lit. We squeezed on to it. A very small seat, and a very safe one.",
      oathbroker: "The crown shows a narrow lit crescent, and under the inverted terms the dark portion is struck: nearly the entire floor. The lit crescent alone is exempt, on the side the crown is turned. The company moved to it at once and remained within it.",
      ironpalm: "A thin bright rim on the crown. Under the turned rule, everything else was struck. The only quiet was that rim. We walked to it together and stood close. It was a narrow room, and a quiet one.",
      ranger: "Crown shows a thin bright rim. Rule's flipped: the dark's what gets cut, so nearly the whole floor is red and the only cover is that rim. Ran to it on the crown's notice and packed in tight. Everybody.",
      shadowblade: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and for once the light was the safe place and the shadow was not, which I resented. Nearly the whole floor was struck. We packed into the bright rim on the side the crown was lit.",
      pyromancer: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and the dark was the part that was struck, which is nearly all of the floor! The only safe place was that thin edge of light. I ran for it, glowing with relief, and we crowded in together.",
      thermist: "Thin lit rim at the edge of the crown. Rule flipped: dark gets cut. Nearly the whole floor red; the rim is the only cover. Run to the lit edge, pack in tight. I ran hot to get there and stayed there, cooling, together with everybody.",
      driftblade: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and the rule turned over so the dark was struck, which was nearly all the floor, and the bright rim was the one place to stand, and I ran for it and wedged in with the rest, packed tight as a flock on a fence.",
      alembist: "Reading: a thin bright rim on the crown, under the inverted rule. Result: the dark is struck, so nearly the whole floor. Dose: all four to the lit rim, on the side the crown is turned, packed close. Smallest safe quantity yet.",
      threadweaver: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and under the turned rule the dark was cut: nearly the whole floor. I drew my stitches to that thin edge of light and packed them in tight. A small seam to stand on, and it held.",
      marionettist: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and under the new rule the dark is what is cut: the whole stage but a sliver of the lit edge. The company packed into that sliver, shoulder to shoulder. (A very small spotlight. Everyone fit.)",
      cleric: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and now the dark is cut, so nearly all of the floor was struck. I called everyone to that thin edge of light and we crowded in together, on the side where the crown was lit. A small refuge, and enough.",
      grovekeeper: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and under the turned rule the dark was struck, nearly the whole field. The only safe ground was the thin lit edge. My lot packed into it like sheep in a lee. We took nothing.",
      chronomancer: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and the rule turned over, so the dark is cut and the bright rim is the shelter. I will have been at that rim before the red showed, and I was, because I read the crown first. We crowded in, close.",
      tidecaller: "A thin bright rim on the moon behind her, and under the turned rule it is the dark that is cut: nearly the whole floor. The thin lit edge was the only dry shore. We crowded onto it, close, and the cut broke everywhere else.",
      lanternkeeper: "A thin bright rim on the crown, and under the turned rule the dark is cut, so the lamp goes in the light, the thin edge of it. I carried it there, and the others crowded round. A very small circle of light, and enough for four.",
      kenseier: "A thin bright rim on the crown: under the turned form, the dark is the stroke, so nearly the whole room. The lit rim alone is shelter. We went to it on the crown's notice, close together, and met the stroke by not being there.",
      houndmaster: "Thin bright rim on the crown, and the rule's flipped, so everything dark gets cut, near the whole floor. Only the lit edge is safe. Dogs and I and the rest packed in there tight, on the side the crown was lit. Tight fit, nobody hurt.",
    } },
    'Umbral Half Moon': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown was half bright, and under the turned rule the dark half was cut. So the bright half was the safe half, and I took it, well back from the line through the middle. Nobody stayed on the dark side out of habit.",
      gloomsworn: "Half the crown bright, half dark, and now the dark half was the one struck. I had to cross into the light, which is not where I live, and I stood well back from the line through the middle. The bright half was kind.",
      thresher: "The crown is half bright, dear, and with the rule turned over it's the dark half that is cut. So pop into the bright half and stay well back from the line through the middle. I like it better when the light is safe.",
      bloodreaver: "Half the crown bright and the dark half got cut, a straight line through the middle of the room! The bright side was safe. I crossed to it and stood a long stride back from the line, and thought how odd it was to be hiding in the light.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a crown half lit under the turned rule: the dark half is struck, so the lit half is the safe one, on the side the crown is turned. The dividing line runs through the centre, so I stood a clear pace inside the lit half.",
      gorgemaw: "She halved the table again, only now it was the dark half that went under the knife, and the bright half stayed on the plate. The line runs through the centrepiece, so I sat a good way back from it, in the bright half.",
      oathbroker: "One half of the floor is charged, and by the inverted terms it is the dark half. The lit half, as the crown shows, is exempt. The dividing line passes through the centre, so I took a position well within the lit half.",
      ironpalm: "Half the crown shone. The turned rule struck the dark half. I walked to the bright half and stood well back from the line through the middle. The dark half was loud, and I was not in it.",
      ranger: "Half the crown lit, rule flipped, so the dark half's the one that gets cut. Safe ground is the bright half, well in from the line through the centre. Moved across to the lit side on the crown's notice, and held.",
      shadowblade: "Half of the crown was bright, and the dark half was struck, which put the shadow on the wrong side of the line. I crossed into the light for once, well back from the middle, and stood in it with as much dignity as I could manage.",
      pyromancer: "Half her crown blazed and it was the DARK half that was cut, so the whole safe side of the room was the bright one! I stood in it, a stride back from the line through the middle, in my element for once, and finished my spell.",
      thermist: "Half the crown lit. Rule flipped, dark half struck. Cross to the bright half, well back from the line through the middle. A little warm for my liking, but safe. I held there, and the cold half took the whole blow.",
      driftblade: "The crown was half bright and under the turned rule the dark half was struck, so I was already running across the line into the light, a long stride past the middle, and the dark half landed behind me, and I stood there and it was bright and I was fine.",
      alembist: "Reading: the crown lit across half, under the inverted rule. Result: the dark half is struck, the lit half is safe. Dose: the lit half only, well back from the line through the centre. The middle is the edge. Say so twice.",
      threadweaver: "The crown was half bright, and now it was the dark half that was cut along the seam through the middle. I drew my stitches over into the bright half, well back from the seam. For once the light was where the strong thread was.",
      marionettist: "The crown lit across half, and under the new rule the dark half of the stage is the one that is cut. The company stood in the bright half, well back from the line through the middle. (The spotlight is the safe place now. Take it.)",
      cleric: "Half of her crown was bright, and the dark half was struck. I asked everyone to cross into the light and stay well back from the line through the middle. It felt odd to be told that the dark was the danger. I said it twice.",
      grovekeeper: "Half the crown in light and half in shadow, and the shadow half was struck, like a hedge fire taking the dark side of the field. We stood on the sunny side, well back from the hedge. It felt strange to flee the shade.",
      chronomancer: "The crown is half lit, and the rule is turned, so the dark half is cut. I will have crossed the middle before it landed; I did, a good way into the bright half. The line through the middle is the edge, and I stayed back from it.",
      tidecaller: "Half the moon behind her bright, and the dark half was cut, a straight causeway down the middle of the room. We waded across into the bright half and stood well back from the line. The dark half took the whole blow.",
      lanternkeeper: "Half the crown was lit, and under the turned rule the dark half was cut. I hung the lamp well inside the bright half, back from the line through the middle, and the others came to it. A lamp is safest where it shines, for once.",
      kenseier: "The crown shows a half, and under the turned form the dark half is the stroke. The bright half is the shelter. I stood a full pace back from the line through the middle, in the light, and the stroke came down on the dark.",
      houndmaster: "Half the crown's bright, and the dark half gets cut, line straight through the middle. Bright half's safe. Dogs and I crossed over and stood well back from the line. Odd, hiding in the light. Worked fine.",
    } },
    'The Last Dance': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Six cuts, one after another, lit and then turned over, and no one place was safe through all of them. The crown showed the next while the last was landing. I called each move before it landed and we kept the walks short. The last was a full moon, and the healer had us ready.",
      gloomsworn: "Six cuts, the lit and the turned-over taking it in turns, and no ground that stayed safe. A man cannot hold a post in this dance, so I stopped trying. I looked at the next crown while the last one landed, walked short, and kept moving. The last was a full moon.",
      thresher: "Six cuts, dear, lit and then turned over, back and forth, so no spot stays safe. Watch the next crown while the last one lands, and take a short walk each time, never a long one. Mind the last, which is a full moon. It rather brings down the house.",
      bloodreaver: "Six cuts in a row, lit and turned over by turns, and no ground stayed safe! I never got to stand and take one. I watched the next crown while the last landed and took short, quick steps. The last was the whole moon, and it hurt in every inch.",
      runeguard: "The sign is six cuts alternating: the lit, then the turned over. No ground is safe for all six, and the crown shows the next cut as the last lands. I read ahead each time and kept the walk short. The sixth is the full moon, and the healer is ready for it.",
      gorgemaw: "Six courses without a pause, lit and turned over by turns, and no seat stayed safe. I watched the next plate while the last was landing, and moved a short way each time. The sixth was the whole moon, and I was filled up for it.",
      oathbroker: "Six claims in succession, the lit and the inverted alternating, and no single position is exempt from all. The crown discloses each next claim as the last falls due. I read ahead and moved the short distance required. The sixth is general, and we were mended for it.",
      ironpalm: "Six words, one after another, lit and then turned over. No place was quiet for all of them. I watched the crown for the next while the last one was still speaking, and took a short step each time. The sixth was the whole moon.",
      ranger: "Six cuts, lit then flipped, alternating. No spot stays clear through all of them. Crown shows the next one as the last lands. Short moves, read ahead, keep walking. Sixth's the full moon, so topped off before it came.",
      shadowblade: "Six cuts, light and then shadow, and no corner stayed mine. I watched the next crown while the last one landed, and slipped a short way each time. Not one of them found me. The sixth was the full moon, which finds everyone.",
      pyromancer: "Six cuts in a row, lit and turned over, and I could not plant my feet for any two of them! I watched the next crown while the last landed and hopped a short way each time. The sixth was the full moon, and I took it standing next to the healer.",
      thermist: "Six cuts, lit and flipped, back and forth. No one place stays clear. Look at the next crown while the last one lands, and take a short step every time. Hot, then cold, then hot. The sixth is a full moon. Topped off beforehand.",
      driftblade: "Six cuts, lit then turned over, one after the other, and no ground stayed safe, which is the only dance I have ever liked, because I never stopped, I read the next crown while the last one landed and slipped to the new dark, short steps, every time, and the sixth was the full moon.",
      alembist: "Reading: six cuts, alternating lit and inverted, no spot safe for all. Dose: read the next crown while the last cut lands, short steps, no long walks. The sixth is a full moon, so keep a strong dose for it. Everything before it is movement.",
      threadweaver: "Six cuts, lit and turned over by turns, and no seam of floor stayed safe through them. I kept my stitches moving a little at a time, looking at the next crown while the last landed. The sixth was the full moon, and I mended everyone to the brim before it.",
      marionettist: "Six cues in a row, the lit and the turned over by turns, and no mark on the stage stays safe. The company read the next crown while the last cue landed and shifted a short way each time. The sixth was the full moon: the finale, and the healer had the company ready.",
      cleric: "Six cuts, lit and turned over by turns, and nowhere stayed safe for long. I asked everyone to look at the next crown while the last was landing, and to take only short steps. The sixth was the full moon, and I had the light on each of them before it.",
      grovekeeper: "Six cuts, sun and shade by turns, so no patch of the field stayed safe. I watched the next crown while the last one landed, and my lot moved a short way each time, never far. The sixth was the full moon, and I'd planted for it.",
      chronomancer: "Six cuts, lit and turned over by turns, and the crown shows the next while the last is landing, so I will have already been moving when it came. Short steps every time. The sixth is a full moon, and I had everyone mended in advance. Early, always.",
      tidecaller: "Six cuts, one after another, the lit and the turned over by turns, like waves from two directions. No shore stayed dry. I watched the next crown while the last broke and we moved a short way each time. The sixth was the full moon, and I raised the water high before it.",
      lanternkeeper: "Six cuts, lit and turned over by turns, so the lamp could not stay in one place. I looked at the next crown while the last was landing and carried it a short way each time, and the others followed the light. The sixth was the full moon, and I had topped us up.",
      kenseier: "Six strokes, the lit and the turned over alternating, and each lands a little sooner. No ground is safe for all. I read the next crown while the last landed and took one short step on each beat. The sixth is the whole moon, and a blade cannot turn it.",
      houndmaster: "Six cuts, one after the other, lit then flipped, and no spot stays safe. Looked at the next crown while the last one landed, short walks, dogs at my knee. Sixth's the whole moon, so got mended first.",
    } },
    'Totality': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The red had covered the whole crown, and she lifted the blade, and everything went white. A shield cannot answer that cast. We had spent too long on our feet and too little on her. Next time every opening is used, and she is down before the crown is red.",
      gloomsworn: "The crown was red all over, and the room held perfectly still, and then it ended us all at once. I have waited at my post through worse. We were too slow, and the red was the clock. Bring her down while the crown still has some light in it.",
      thresher: "The little moon went red all over, dear, and the room held its breath, and then it was the whole party's hour. We had dallied. Hit her in every pause, and have her down while the crown still has a bit of white in it.",
      bloodreaver: "The crown went red all over and she raised the blade and took every one of us in one breath, and I did not even get to bleed for it! We were too slow. Hit harder, hit more, and finish her before the red has the whole crown.",
      runeguard: "The red covered the crown, and the entry is plain: the cast that follows reaches all and cannot be warded. We had not finished her. A faster party strikes in every pause and spends fewer steps on the floor. The red on the crown is the clock; read it.",
      gorgemaw: "The crown went red all over, the room held still, and the last course was served to every chair at once, and I was not done with the one before it. A slow table gets cleared. Eat faster, and have her finished before the red has the whole crown.",
      oathbroker: "The red covered the crown and the term expired, and the final cast fell upon every party at once. Our performance fell short: too much time spent dodging, too little striking. Next time, strike in every opening, and have the account settled before the crown is red.",
      ironpalm: "The crown went red and the room went still, and then she spoke once, to everyone, and it was the last word. We had been slow. Next time, fewer wasted steps, a harder strike in every pause, and her down before the red reaches the end of the crown.",
      ranger: "Crown went red all over. Cast came, whole party. Too slow: careful steps where there should have been shots. Hit her whenever there's an opening. Finish her before the red covers the crown. No time left after.",
      shadowblade: "The red covered the crown, and every shadow in the room went out at once, mine included. We had been too polite about the floor. Strike whenever she is open, and have her down before the crown is red. Not even I can slip that cast.",
      pyromancer: "The crown went red all over, and the whole room went white, and every one of us went with it! My spells were too few and too shy. Burn harder, burn longer, and finish her before the red covers the crown.",
      thermist: "The red covered the crown. Then the cast, the whole party at once. We were slower than the red. Too many steps wasted on dodging. Strike in every opening, and finish early. I ran cold and then I ran out.",
      driftblade: "The crown went red all over and the cast came and I ran, of course I ran, and there was nowhere to run to, because we had spent too long dodging and not long enough striking. Next time, fewer laps and harder hits, and her down before the red.",
      alembist: "Cause: the crown goes red and the final cast reaches everyone. Cure: finish her before it does. Note: our dose was too slow, and we spent too many openings on nothing. Label: this batch was spoiled, and the red was the expiry date.",
      threadweaver: "The crown went red all over and the cast cut every thread at once. We had been too slow, and too much time was spent on the floor. Next time, hit her harder in every opening, and finish her before the crown is covered.",
      marionettist: "The crown went fully red, the house went silent, and the curtain came down on all of us. We had played the dodging too carefully and the finale too late. Strike in every pause, and be done before the crown is red. (The critics were kinder than we deserved.)",
      cleric: "The red covered the whole crown, and she lifted the blade, and every one of my friends fell. I had mended a great deal and urged them too little. Next time I will tell them to strike harder, and to finish her while there is still white on the crown.",
      grovekeeper: "The crown went red like the last leaf on a bare tree, and the cast came, and took the whole field. We had dithered over the floor, and the season ran out. Next time, strike hard in every opening, and be done before the red covers the crown.",
      chronomancer: "The red covered the crown, the hush came, and then it landed on all of us, however often I tried to undo it. We were too slow, too careful. Next time we hit harder in every opening, and she is down before the crown is covered.",
      tidecaller: "The crown went red, and the room held still as a flat sea, and then the cast came in like a flood that does not turn. The water had risen while we lingered over the floor. Next time, quicker, and harder in every gap, before the red.",
      lanternkeeper: "The crown went red all over, and every lamp in the room went out at once. We had spent too many wicks on careful steps and too few on striking. Next time, hit her in every opening, and have her down while the crown still has white in it.",
      kenseier: "The crown went red, and she lifted the blade, and the stroke had no beat to meet and no edge to turn. We arrived at it behind schedule. We spent our strokes on caution, and she spent hers on us. Next time, finish her before the crown is covered.",
      houndmaster: "Crown went red all over and the whole lot of us went down with it, dogs and all. Too much time spent on the dodging and too little on the biting. Next time, hit hard whenever she's open, and have her finished before the red covers the crown.",
    } },
    'Two Crowns': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two crowns this time: hers behind her head, and a smaller one in the dust at her feet, each with its own shape and turn. Both cuts landed together. Report: the safe ground is only what neither one lights, so I read both before I moved.",
      gloomsworn: "Her reflection in the dust wore a crown of its own, turned some other way. Two cuts, both landing together. The only safe ground was dark in both. I read the high crown and then the low one, and stood where the two dark parts overlapped.",
      thresher: "She has a reflection in the dust, dear, and it wears a little crown of its own, with a different shape and turn! Both cuts land together. So find the ground that is dark in both crowns, and stand there. Read twice before you walk.",
      bloodreaver: "A second crown in the dust at her feet, a different shape, a different turn, and both cuts landed together! One crown would have been plenty. The only safe ground was where both were dark. I read both and went there, and was not cut once.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a second crown, small, in the dust at her feet, with a shape and a turn of its own. Both cuts are painted and land together. The safe ground is what neither crown lights. I read the high crown, then the low, and set the party where the dark of both agreed.",
      gorgemaw: "Two crowns, one on her head and one reflected in the dust, each with its own shape and turn, and two dishes landing at once. The only unserved ground was dark in both. I read the first plate, then the second, and sat where both were bare.",
      oathbroker: "A second crown appears in the dust, with a shape and a turn of its own, and both claims fall due at once. Only what neither crown lights is exempt. I read the one above, then the one below, and took my position where both disclosed darkness.",
      ironpalm: "Two crowns, one above and one in the dust, each speaking in its own shape and turn, and both cuts landed at once. Only the ground dark in both was quiet. I read the first, then the second, and stood where they agreed.",
      ranger: "Second crown in the dust, its own shape and turn. Both cuts land together. Safe ground is where both crowns are dark. Read the high one, read the low one, find the overlap, move early. Not much floor left, so don't guess.",
      shadowblade: "She has a reflection in the dust, and it wears a crown of its own, turned some other way, which seemed greedy. Both cuts landed together. I read both, found the shadow they shared, and stood in it. Not a lot of room.",
      pyromancer: "A second crown appeared in the dust at her feet, a different shape, a different turn, and both cuts fell at once! Two spotlights, and I was to find the one place neither shone. I read both and stood in the gap.",
      thermist: "Two crowns: hers, and a small one in the dust, each its own shape and turn. Both cuts together. Safe ground only where both are dark. Read twice, then move. Cold logic, no guessing. I found the overlap and stood in it.",
      driftblade: "A second crown in the dust at her feet, turned some other way, and both cuts coming down together, so the only safe ground was dark in both, and I read both crowns and ran to where they agreed and it was not much floor, but it was enough.",
      alembist: "Reading: two crowns, hers and a reflection in the dust, each with its own shape and turn. Result: both cuts land together. Dose: stand where neither crown lights. Read both before moving, since the overlap is small.",
      threadweaver: "Two crowns, hers and a small one in the dust, each cut on its own pattern, and both cuts landing together. The safe ground is only where both patterns leave the floor bare. I read both and drew my stitches to the overlap.",
      marionettist: "Two spotlights this scene: her crown, and a small second one in the dust at her feet, each with its own shape and turn. Both cuts land at once. The company stood on the one mark that neither light reached. (Read both before the entrance.)",
      cleric: "A second crown shone in the dust at her feet, with a different shape and turn, and both cuts landed together. I read both and asked everyone to stand where neither one was lit. It is a small place, and it needs two looks.",
      grovekeeper: "She cast a second shadow, a small crown in the dust at her feet, with a different shape and turn. Both cuts fell at once. The only safe ground was what neither lit, like the patch left under two clouds. I read both and we stood there.",
      chronomancer: "Two crowns, and both cuts land together. Hers, and a small one in the dust, each with its own shape and turn. The safe ground is only what neither lights. I will have read both before the cast was over, and stood in the overlap.",
      tidecaller: "Two moons: hers, and a small one reflected in the dust like a moon in a pool, each with its own shape and turn. Both cuts broke together. The dry ground was only what neither lit. I read both and we waited there.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two crowns, hers and one in the dust, each lit its own way. Both cuts landed together, so the lamp had to go where both were dark. It was a small patch. I looked at the high crown, then the low, and carried it to the overlap.",
      kenseier: "Two crowns and two strokes, landing together: hers, and a small one in the dust, each with its own shape and turn. Only the ground dark in both is shelter. I read the first form, then the second, and stood where they met.",
      houndmaster: "Second crown in the dust at her feet, own shape and turn, and both cuts land together. Safe ground's what neither lights. Read the high one, read the low one, found the gap. Dogs and I stood in it. Small, but it holds.",
    } },
    'Annular': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown went full, with a dark middle. The floor was struck in a ring, leaving the very middle and a band at the wall. We took the middle, close together. The ring is wide, so I told the line not to stand halfway between.",
      gloomsworn: "A full crown with a dark hollow in it, and the floor struck in a ring: the middle spared, the rim spared. I took the middle, which was home to me, and the rest followed. Nobody stood in the wide band between.",
      thresher: "A full crown with a dark little hollow in the middle, dear, so the floor gets a ring cut through it, and only the very middle and the band at the wall are spared. Pick one, either one, and don't linger between.",
      bloodreaver: "A full crown with a hole in it, and the floor cut in a great ring! The middle was safe and so was the rim at the wall. I could have taken either. I took the middle with my friends, and the ring went off around us.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a full crown with a dark middle. The floor is struck in a wide ring, sparing the centre and the band at the wall. Both are safe. The centre is closer, and holds all four, so I held the party there.",
      gorgemaw: "A great ring of pastry, with a hole in the middle and crust at the rim, and the ring was what got carved. The hole and the crust stayed whole. I stood in the hole, which is a fine place for a stomach.",
      oathbroker: "A full crown with a dark middle declares a ring charged upon the floor, exempting the centre and the band at the wall. Either exemption suffices. I removed the company to the centre and held them there until it fell.",
      ironpalm: "A full crown with a dark middle. The floor spoke in a wide ring, and left the centre quiet and the rim quiet. We stood in the centre, together. The ring spoke around us.",
      ranger: "Full crown, dark middle. Ring across the floor, centre spared, wall band spared. Went to the centre, close in, all four. The ring's wide: don't stop in it. Either end works, but the centre's nearer.",
      shadowblade: "A full crown with a dark hole in the middle, and the ring cut through the floor around it, sparing the centre and the rim. I took the centre, where nothing was looking, and the ring went off around me like applause.",
      pyromancer: "A full crown with a black hole in the middle, and the floor cut in a great bright ring around it! The very centre was spared, and so was a band at the wall. I took the centre and stood in my own small, safe spotlight.",
      thermist: "Full crown, dark hollow in the middle. Floor cut in a wide ring. Centre safe, wall band safe. Go to the centre with the others, close, and stay. Don't stop halfway: that's the hot part. I ran in, and cooled.",
      driftblade: "A full crown with a dark hollow, and the floor cut in a ring through all the room between the middle and the wall, and I ran for the middle, and stood in it, and not halfway, because halfway is nowhere at all.",
      alembist: "Reading: a full crown with a dark middle. Result: a ring struck across the floor, sparing the centre and a band at the wall. Dose: all four to the centre, close, or to the wall band. Not the space between.",
      threadweaver: "A full crown with a dark hollow in it, and the floor cut in a ring, sparing the middle and the band by the wall. I drew my stitches to the middle and knotted them close. The ring went round us.",
      marionettist: "A full crown with a dark middle, and a ring of light cut across the stage, sparing centre stage and the band at the very wall. The company took centre stage, shoulder to shoulder. (The ring is wide. Do not make your exit halfway.)",
      cleric: "The crown was full with a dark middle, and a ring of light was cut across the floor, sparing the centre and the band at the wall. I called everyone into the middle and held them there. Nobody stood halfway between.",
      grovekeeper: "A full crown with a dark hollow, and a ring cut through the field like a fairy ring, sparing the middle and the rim at the fence. We stood in the middle, all of my lot, close together. The ring burned itself out around us.",
      chronomancer: "A full crown with a dark hollow in the middle: the ring cuts everything between the centre and the wall. I will have been at the centre already. We stood in it, close, and the ring went off around us.",
      tidecaller: "A full moon with a dark middle, and a ring of light cut across the floor like a reef, sparing the centre and the shallows at the wall. We stood in the middle, close together. The ring broke round us and not on us.",
      lanternkeeper: "A full crown with a dark hollow, so the lamp went in the middle, where it was dark and spared, and the others gathered round it. The floor was cut in a wide ring and the centre and the rim at the wall were left.",
      kenseier: "A full crown with a dark middle: the stroke is a ring, and it spares the centre and the rim. The centre is the nearer shelter. We stood there, close, and the stroke came down on the floor between us and the wall.",
      houndmaster: "Full crown, dark hole in the middle. Floor cut in a ring, centre spared, wall band spared. Whistled the dogs into the middle and stood tight. Don't stop in the ring. Went off round us like a hoop.",
    } },
    'Libration': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The crown showed a shape, and I started to move, and then it rocked a quarter turn and stopped. The cut followed the second turn, not the first. I learned to wait for it to settle. The floor was painted after, with time to spare.",
      gloomsworn: "The crown showed a shape, and then it rocked a quarter turn, once, and stopped, as a tired man turns in his sleep. The cut followed where it stopped. I did not commit to the first turn. I waited, and then walked.",
      thresher: "The little moon fibs, dear: it shows one turn, then rocks a quarter way round, once, and stops. The cut lands where it stops. So don't run at the first showing. Wait for it to settle, and then there is plenty of time.",
      bloodreaver: "The crown showed a shape and I charged for the dark, and then it rocked a quarter turn and I charged the wrong way! The cut lands at the second turn. Wait for it to settle. I hate waiting. I waited.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a crown shown at one turn that then rocks a quarter turn and settles. The cut lands at the second turn, and the floor is painted from the settling, with ample time. The first reading is a draft. I did not move until the crown had stopped.",
      gorgemaw: "The crown set one shape on the table, then turned the dish a quarter way round and left it there. The carving followed the second turn. I did not rise at the first showing. I waited for the dish to be set down, and then moved.",
      oathbroker: "The crown discloses a shape, then rocks a quarter turn and fixes. The cut follows the fixed turn, not the first. The floor is painted from the fixing, with ample notice. The first disclosure is provisional. I waited until the crown was settled.",
      ironpalm: "The crown said one thing, then turned a quarter way and said another, once, and stopped. The cut followed the second word. I did not move on the first. I waited for the crown to settle, and the floor told me the rest.",
      ranger: "Crown shows a shape, then rocks a quarter turn, once, and stops. Cut follows where it stops. Don't move on the first look. Wait until it locks; the floor paints with time to spare. Early walkers walk wrong.",
      shadowblade: "The crown showed me one thing and then rocked a quarter turn and showed me another, which is the sort of trick I respect. The cut follows the second. I waited in the dark, patiently, until it settled, and then stepped where the floor said.",
      pyromancer: "The crown showed a shape and I was off, and then it rocked a quarter turn and I was off the wrong way! The cut follows the second turn. Stand still until it stops rocking, and the floor tells you the rest. I resent patience.",
      thermist: "Crown shows a shape, rocks a quarter turn, stops. The cut lands at the second turn. I ran hot on the first look and had to turn back, and then cooled. Wait until it settles. The floor paints with time to spare.",
      driftblade: "The crown showed a shape and I was already running, and then it rocked a quarter turn and stopped and I was running the wrong way, so now I wait for the crown to settle, which I hate, and then run, and there is plenty of time.",
      alembist: "Reading: the crown shows a shape, then rocks a quarter turn, once, and settles. Result: the cut follows the settled turn. Dose: no movement until it settles. The floor is painted from the settling, with ample time.",
      threadweaver: "The crown showed one pattern, then rocked a quarter turn and stopped, and the cut followed the second. I held my stitches still until it settled, then moved them. The floor was painted after, with plenty of time.",
      marionettist: "The crown gives one cue, then rocks a quarter turn and settles on another, and the cut follows the second. The company waited for the crown to settle before they moved. (An early entrance is a wrong one.) The floor was painted after, with time to spare.",
      cleric: "The crown showed a shape, and I almost called everyone to move, and then it rocked a quarter turn and stopped. The cut follows the second turn. I learned to wait for it to settle. The floor paints afterward, with time to spare.",
      grovekeeper: "The crown showed one shape, then swung a quarter turn like a weathervane, once, and stopped. The cut followed where it stopped. I waited for the wind to settle before my lot moved. The floor was painted after, with time to spare.",
      chronomancer: "The crown shows one turn, then rocks a quarter way and settles. I will have moved at the first look and been wrong. The cut follows the second turn. Wait for it to settle; the floor is painted after, with plenty of time.",
      tidecaller: "The moon behind her showed a shape, then rocked a quarter turn like a boat on a swell and stopped. The cut follows where it settles. I waited until the water was still, and then we moved. The floor was painted after, with time to spare.",
      lanternkeeper: "The crown showed a shape, then rocked a quarter turn and settled. I had already picked the dark and had to pick again. The cut follows the second turn. Wait for the crown to settle, then carry the lamp. The floor is painted with time to spare.",
      kenseier: "The crown shows a shape, then rocks a quarter turn, once, and stops. That is a feint, and the stroke follows the second form. I did not answer the first. When the crown settled and the floor was painted, I moved, with time in hand.",
      houndmaster: "Crown showed a shape, then rocked a quarter turn and stopped. Cut follows the second turn, not the first. Nearly sent the dogs the wrong way. Wait for it to settle, floor paints after, plenty of time. Sat the dogs till then.",
    } },
    'Moonfall': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "In the middle of the steps a small moon fell on one of us and stayed where he left it. He took it far from the rest, set it down, and left it. When the light came back it burst, and the painted circle gave us time to stand clear.",
      gloomsworn: "A moon fell on one of us in the middle of the dark, a small round mark over his head, and stayed where he set it down. He walked it far from the rest of us. When the light returned it burst. I stood well clear of the circle.",
      thresher: "A little moon falls on one of us in the middle of the dark, dear, and it's set down wherever they stop. So take it far away from everyone and leave it. When the light comes back it bursts, so don't stand near it. The circle is painted.",
      bloodreaver: "A moon fell on one of my friends in the dark, round and bright and following him about, and then it stayed where he stopped! He took it far from the rest of us. When the light came back it went off, and the red circle told us where.",
      runeguard: "A small round sign over one head in the middle of the dance: a moon follows its bearer and is set down where the bearer stops. The bearer walks it far from the party. It bursts when the light returns, and the circle is painted. We stood clear.",
      gorgemaw: "A little moon was dropped on one guest in the middle of the dark, and wherever they stood when the cast ended, there it sat. They took it far from the rest of us. When the light came back, it went off, and I was nowhere near the dish.",
      oathbroker: "In the midst of the dance a moon is charged to one party and is deposited where they stand. The party must remove it far from the others. It is discharged when the light returns, in a painted circle, and the company stood clear.",
      ironpalm: "In the middle of the dark a small moon fell on one of us. He walked it far from the rest and set it down. It waited. When the light came back it spoke, in a painted circle, and we stood clear of it.",
      ranger: "Moon on one hero mid-dance, round mark over the head, follows, sets down where they stop. Carry it far from everybody. Bursts when the light returns, circle painted. Stood off it. Easy, if the carrier goes wide.",
      shadowblade: "A small moon found one of us in the dark and followed him about, and stayed where he left it. He took it a long way from the rest, which is the polite thing to do with a bomb. When the light returned it burst, in a circle we could see.",
      pyromancer: "A little moon dropped on one of us in the dark, a bright round mark, and followed them about until it was set down! They took it a long way from the rest of us. It burst when the light came back, and I kept out of the circle.",
      thermist: "Moon on one hero in the middle of the dark. Follows them, sets down where they stop. Take it far from the others. Bursts when the light returns, in a painted circle. I stood off it. Careful, wide, no hurry.",
      driftblade: "A moon on one of us in the middle of the dark, following them about, and I was not the one so I ran wide of the carrier while he took it far from everyone and set it down, and when the light came back it burst, and I was nowhere near.",
      alembist: "Cause: a moon falls on one hero mid-dance and is set down where they stop. Cure: the carrier takes it far from the party. It bursts when the light returns, in a painted circle. Everyone else stays clear of the carrier and the circle.",
      threadweaver: "A small moon fell on one of my stitches in the middle of the dark, and followed them until it was set down. I cut them loose from the rest and sent them far away. It burst when the light came back, and we kept out of the circle.",
      marionettist: "In the middle of the dark scene a prop moon is dropped on one player, who carries it off and sets it down. They took it far from the company. When the lights came up it burst, in a painted circle, and the company kept clear of it.",
      cleric: "A moon fell on one of my friends in the dark and stayed where they set it down. I asked them to carry it far from the rest of us, and they did. When the light came back it burst, and we stood well clear of the painted circle.",
      grovekeeper: "A small moon fell on one of my lot in the middle of the dark, like a seed set in the field, and stayed where they stopped. They took it far from the rest. When the light came back it burst. We stood clear of the circle.",
      chronomancer: "A moon falls on one hero in the middle of the dark, and is set down where they stop. I will have known who to send far away; I did. It bursts when the light returns, in a painted circle, and we were not in it.",
      tidecaller: "A small moon fell on one of us in the dark like a stone into a pool, and stayed where they set it down. They carried it far from the rest of us. When the light returned, it burst, and we stood clear of the ring.",
      lanternkeeper: "A moon fell on one of my friends in the dark. They carried it far from the rest of us and set it down. When the light came back it burst, and I kept the others out of the painted circle. One carrier, far away, one burst.",
      kenseier: "In the middle of the sword dance, a moon falls on one of us and is left where they stop. The carrier walks it far from the rest. It bursts when the light returns, in a painted circle. We stood clear, and the blade stayed sheathed.",
      houndmaster: "Small moon on one of us in the middle of the dark, round mark over the head, set down where he stopped. Took it a long way from the rest. Burst when the light came back, red circle painted. Dogs and I stayed clear.",
    } },
    'Harvest': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The dance after the moonlets had a new name, Harvest. A moon that one of its cuts has lit bursts far wider. So the carriers set theirs where no cut would reach, on the dark side, and the line stood off them. I kept my distance.",
      gloomsworn: "Harvest, they call the dance after the moons. A moon that a cut has already lit goes off wide when the last one lands. We set ours on the dark side, where no cut would reach. I kept clear of both and waited.",
      thresher: "When moons are about, dear, the waxing dance is called Harvest, and a moon an earlier cut has lit bursts much wider than one left in the dark. Put yours on the dark side, where no cut reaches, and the rest of us keep clear.",
      bloodreaver: "Twice as wide! That is how far a moon bursts when one of the cuts of Harvest has already lit it. I wanted to see it and was talked out of it. The carriers set theirs on the dark side, and I stood well back.",
      runeguard: "The rule is new here, and I enter it in the margin: in the dance named Harvest, a moon that an earlier cut has lit bursts wider than one left dark. Therefore the moons belong on the dark side, where no cut of the dance will touch them.",
      gorgemaw: "Harvest is the dance served after the moons, and a moon that a cut has already warmed boils over far wider. So the carriers set theirs on the dark side, where the knife never reaches, and I sat clear of them.",
      oathbroker: "The dance following the moons is Harvest, and a moon already lit by a cut discharges at greater extent. The carriers must therefore deposit theirs upon the dark side, where no cut of the dance falls. I kept clear of both.",
      ironpalm: "Harvest follows the moons. A moon a cut has already lit speaks far louder at the end. The carriers set theirs on the dark side, where no cut reached, and the rest of us stayed back. A small voice does small harm.",
      ranger: "Moonlets, then Harvest. Any moon a cut's already lit goes wide at the end. Carriers dropped theirs on the dark side, where no cut lands. Gave them room.",
      shadowblade: "They call the dance Harvest when moons are about. A moon a cut has lit bursts wide at the end, and one left in the shadow does not. I told the carriers to leave theirs in the shadow, and kept my own distance.",
      pyromancer: "A moon that one of the cuts has already lit goes off MUCH wider in Harvest! Imagine being outshone by your own moon. The carriers set theirs on the dark side, where no cut lands, and I stood well back, being modest.",
      thermist: "Harvest: the dance after the moons. A moon a cut has already lit bursts far wider, and a wide burst runs hot. Carriers set theirs on the dark side, where nothing lands. I stood back from them both.",
      driftblade: "After the moons came Harvest, and a moon that one of the cuts had lit would burst wide, which leaves no room to run, so the carriers took theirs to the dark side where no cut would reach, and I stayed well away from both.",
      alembist: "Cause: the dance after Moonlets is Harvest. Result: a moon already lit by an earlier cut bursts wide at the end. Cure: place the moons where no cut falls, on the dark side, and keep everyone else off them.",
      threadweaver: "In Harvest the moons are the loose threads: any one a cut has already lit unravels much wider. I sent my two carriers to the dark side, where no cut touches, and told the others to keep well back from them.",
      marionettist: "The dance after the moonlets is billed as Harvest, and any prop moon that a cut has already lit goes off far wider. The carriers placed theirs on the dark side, where no cut reaches. (A prop in the lights is a prop that explodes.)",
      cleric: "The dance after the moonlets is Harvest, and a moon already lit by one of the cuts bursts much wider. I asked the carriers to set theirs on the dark side, where none of the cuts reach, and I kept everyone else off them.",
      grovekeeper: "Harvest follows the moons. A moon lit by an early cut ripens wide, and bursts wide, at the end. So the carriers set theirs on the dark side, where no cut falls, and the rest of my lot kept off them.",
      chronomancer: "Harvest, the dance after the moonlets, and a moon that a cut has lit bursts wider at the end. I will have told the carriers to leave theirs in the dark; they did, and I kept my distance from both.",
      tidecaller: "Harvest is the dance after the moons, and a moon the cuts have already lit bursts far wider than one left in the dark. The carriers set theirs on the dark side, where no cut breaks, and we stayed off them like boats off a reef.",
      lanternkeeper: "A lamp left in the light gets blown out, and a moon left in the light bursts wide: that is Harvest, the dance after the moons. The carriers set theirs on the dark side, where no cut falls, and the rest of us kept off them.",
      kenseier: "Harvest is the dance after the moons: a moon already lit by a cut bursts wider at the end. The carriers set theirs on the dark side, where no stroke lands, and the rest of us stood off them. A wide burst has no edge to turn.",
      houndmaster: "Dance after the moonlets is Harvest. Any moon a cut's lit goes off wide. Carriers set theirs on the dark side, where no cut lands, and the rest of us kept off them. Wide burst'd catch a dog quick.",
    } },
    'Moon-Touched': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of us carried an eye overhead, and for a while the cuts struck them the other way round: the dark harmed them and the light did not. They stood inside the red when it was painted, and the rest of us stood outside. Blasts and dashes still struck them as ever.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of us bore an eye over our heads, and for a long while the rule was reversed for them: the painted red was safe, and the dark was not. They stood in the red, and I stood in the dark. Blasts and dashes were no kinder to them.",
      thresher: "Two of us wear a little eye, dear, and for a good while the cuts treat them backwards: standing in the red is safe, and standing in the dark is not. So they step into the paint, and the rest of us step out. Blasts and dashes still find them.",
      bloodreaver: "Two of my friends wore an eye and the cuts struck them backwards for a good while! The red was their safe place and the dark was their wound. They stood in the paint and grinned. Blasts, dashes and bursts still found them as they found me.",
      runeguard: "The sign is an eye over two heads. For a time the cuts strike them the other way round: they are safe inside the painted shape and struck outside it. They stood in the red; the rest stood in the dark. All else strikes them as before.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests wore an eye, and the carving was reversed for them: the painted portion was their safe seat, and the bare floor was not. They sat in the red. The rest of us sat in the dark. The blasts and the dashes still reached them.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties bear an eye, and the cuts are inverted in their case for a term: the painted shape exempts them and the bare floor does not. They remained within the red, and the rest of us stood outside it. Blasts, dashes and bursts apply to them as before.",
      ironpalm: "Two of us wore an eye. For a time the cuts spoke to them backwards: the painted red was safe and the dark was not. They stood in the red, and I stood in the dark. Everything else struck them as it struck us.",
      ranger: "Eye on two heroes. Cuts hit them backwards for a good while: safe inside the painted shape, hurt outside. They step into the red, the rest of us stay out. Blasts, dashes and bursts still hit them normal. Mind the mark running out.",
      shadowblade: "Two of us wore an eye, which turned the rule over for them: the painted red was safe, and the shadow was where they got hurt. A cruel joke on the shadowy. They stood in the paint, and I stood in the dark. All else struck them as before.",
      pyromancer: "Two of us wore a glowing eye, and for a good while the cuts struck them BACKWARDS: light was safe and the dark was not! They stood in the paint, grinning. I stood in the dark with everyone else. Blasts and dashes still found them.",
      thermist: "Eye on two heroes, and the cuts hit them backwards for a good while: safe in the painted red, struck in the dark. They step into the paint, the rest of us stay out. Blasts, dashes and bursts hit them as normal. The mark runs out.",
      driftblade: "Two of us wore an eye and for a good while the cuts hit them backwards, so they ran into the paint while I ran out of it, which is a very odd thing to do, and the blasts and the dashes still found them just the same.",
      alembist: "Cause: an eye on two heroes. Effect: for a long while the cuts strike them in reverse, safe in the painted shape, hurt outside it. Cure: they stand in the red, everyone else stands outside. Blasts, dashes and bursts are unchanged.",
      threadweaver: "Two of my stitches wore an eye, and for a good while the cuts struck them in reverse: safe inside the painted shape, struck outside it. They stepped into the red and the rest of us stepped out. Blasts and dashes still struck them as ever.",
      marionettist: "Two players carry an eye, and for a good while their blocking is reversed: the painted mark is their safe spot, and the dark is not. They step into the spotlight, and the rest of the company step out. (Blasts and dashes still find them.)",
      cleric: "Two of my friends wore an eye, and I could not mend a wrong step if they made one. For a good while the cuts struck them backwards, so they stood in the paint while the rest of us kept to the dark. Blasts and dashes were as unkind to them as ever.",
      grovekeeper: "Two of my lot wore an eye, and for a good while the weather was backwards for them: the painted red was safe and the dark was not. They stood in the red and the rest of us stood in the dark. Blasts, dashes and bursts still found them.",
      chronomancer: "Two heroes wear an eye, and for a good while the cuts strike them backwards: safe inside the painted shape, hurt outside it. They step into the red and I will have stepped out. Blasts and dashes still find them, as they find anyone.",
      tidecaller: "Two of us wore an eye, and for a good while the sea ran backwards for them: the painted red was calm water and the dark was the surf. They stood in the red, and we stood out. Blasts and dashes broke on them as on anyone.",
      lanternkeeper: "An eye hung over two of my friends, and for a good while the cuts struck them in reverse. I set a lamp inside each painted shape for them to stand by, and kept the others out in the dark. Blasts and dashes found them the same as anyone.",
      kenseier: "Two of us carry an eye, and for a time the stroke is turned about for them: safe inside the painted shape, struck outside it. They stood in the red, and the rest of us stood out. Blasts, dashes and bursts land on them as on anyone.",
      houndmaster: "Eye over two of us, and for a good while the cuts hit them backwards: safe in the painted red, hurt in the dark. They stepped into the paint, rest of us stayed out, dogs too. Blasts and dashes still found them same as ever.",
    } },
    'Blood Moon Dance': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "One cut of the dance came with a red limb on the crown, and that cut was turned over: it struck the dark. The far edge we had hidden on was the one place not to stand. We stepped in off it for that cut, and the rest of the dance ran as always.",
      gloomsworn: "A red limb on the crown marked one cut as turned over, so it struck the dark and spared the light. My usual dark corner was the one place not to be. I read which cut it was, stepped out of the dark for it, and no further than I had to.",
      thresher: "One cut in the dance has a red limb on the crown, dear, and that cut is turned over: it strikes the dark and spares the light. So the far dark rim you've been tucked into is where not to be when it lands. Step out of it, then tuck back in.",
      bloodreaver: "One cut had a red limb on the crown, and it struck the dark and spared the light, the wrong way round! The thin edge I had been hiding in was the one place not to be. I moved off it in time, and loved the trick. The rest of the dance ran as usual.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a red limb on the crown: that cut is turned over, striking the dark and sparing the light. The usual dark refuge is therefore struck for that one cut. I read the order, left the refuge before it landed, and kept the rest of the dance as written.",
      gorgemaw: "One course came with a red limb on the crown, and it was served backwards: the dark part was carved and the bright part spared. The corner I had been sitting in was the one place not to be. I moved for that dish alone, and went on as before.",
      oathbroker: "One cut of the dance bears a red limb on the crown and is inverted: the dark portion is struck and the lit portion spared. The usual refuge is thereby charged for that one cut. I vacated it in good time, and observed the remaining terms as before.",
      ironpalm: "One cut came with a red limb on the crown, and it spoke backwards: it struck the dark and spared the light. The thin dark edge I had stood in was the loudest place. I stepped out of it for that cut alone, and the rest of the dance was as it had been.",
      ranger: "Red limb on the crown means that cut's flipped: hits the dark, spares the light. Far dark edge, my usual spot, is the one place to avoid for it. Read which cut, left the edge in time, kept the rest of the dance the same.",
      shadowblade: "One cut had a red limb on the crown, and it struck the dark and spared the light, so my favourite shadow was the one place not to be, which seemed pointed. I stepped out of it for that cut alone, and slipped back in afterwards.",
      pyromancer: "One cut came with a red limb on the crown and it was turned over! It struck the dark and spared the light, so the thin dark edge I had been loving was exactly the wrong place. I stepped out for that one and was furious about it.",
      thermist: "Red limb on the crown: that cut is flipped. Dark struck, light spared. The far dark rim, my usual spot, is the one place not to be for it. Read which cut, step off the rim in time, rest of the dance is the same. I ran hot, then settled.",
      driftblade: "One cut had a red limb on the crown and struck the dark and spared the light, so the far dark edge was the wrong place for that one cut, and I was already off it and running before it landed, and back on it for the rest, and not once did I stop.",
      alembist: "Reading: a red limb on the crown marks one cut of the dance as inverted. Result: it strikes the dark and spares the light. Dose: leave the usual dark refuge for that cut alone, and return to it for the rest.",
      threadweaver: "One cut of the dance had a red limb on the crown, and that cut was turned over: it cut the dark and spared the light. The thin dark edge was the one place not to stand for it. I drew my stitches off it in time, and the rest ran as always.",
      marionettist: "One cue in the dance carries a red limb on the crown: it is played backwards, striking the dark and sparing the light. The far wing, our usual mark, is the one place not to be. The company stepped out for that cue alone. (The rest of the act is as written.)",
      cleric: "One cut of the dance had a red limb on the crown, and that cut struck the dark and spared the light, so the dark corner where we had been hiding was the one place not to be. I called everyone out of it for that cut alone.",
      grovekeeper: "One cut had a red limb on the crown, and it turned the weather over: the dark was struck and the light spared. The thin dark edge where we had stood was the one place to leave for it. My lot stepped out in good time, and the rest ran as usual.",
      chronomancer: "The crown has a red limb on one cut, and that cut is turned over: it strikes the dark and spares the light. I will have left the dark edge before it landed, for that cut alone. The rest of the dance stays as it was.",
      tidecaller: "One cut had a red limb on the moon behind her, and it ran the tide backwards: the dark was struck and the light spared. The dry rim we had been standing on was the wet place for that cut. We stepped off it in good time.",
      lanternkeeper: "One cut of the dance had a red limb on the crown, and that cut struck the dark and spared the light. So the lamp could not stay in its dark corner. I carried it out for that cut alone, and the others followed, and then back.",
      kenseier: "One stroke carries a red limb on the crown: it is turned about, striking the dark and sparing the light. The dark edge was the one place not to stand for it. I read the order, left the edge in time, and met the other strokes as before.",
      houndmaster: "One cut had a red limb on the crown, and that one's flipped: hits the dark, spares the light. Far dark rim, where we'd been sat, was the wrong place for it. Whistled the dogs off it in good time. Rest of the dance same as ever.",
    } },
    'Blood Harvest': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "One cut of the dance was turned over, and it struck the far dark edge, the old safe place, so a moon left there would be lit and burst wide. The carriers set theirs short of the edge, in the dark half, and the line stood between them.",
      gloomsworn: "The dance after the moons, with one cut turned over. A moon that any cut lights bursts wide, and the turned cut lights the far dark edge. So the carriers set theirs in the dark half, short of the edge, apart from each other, and I kept well clear.",
      thresher: "A dance with moons about and one cut turned over, dear: that cut strikes the far rim, so the carriers mustn't leave theirs there. They set them in the dark half, a little short of the rim, and the rest of us tiptoe between.",
      bloodreaver: "Moons and a turned cut together, and I love a pair of tricks! The far rim gets struck, so a moon left on it goes off wide. The carriers set theirs short of the rim, in the dark half, apart, and I stood clear of the lot.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a red limb on the crown in a dance that follows the moons. The turned cut strikes the far dark edge; a moon set there is lit and bursts wide. The entry for the carriers: the dark half, short of the edge, apart.",
      gorgemaw: "A dance after the moons, one course served backwards. The backwards course carves the far dark rim, so a moon left there boils over wide. The carriers sat theirs in the dark half but short of the rim, apart, and the rest of us stayed clear.",
      oathbroker: "The dance following the moons includes one inverted cut, which strikes the far dark edge, so a moon deposited there is lit and discharges wide. The carriers deposit theirs within the dark half, short of the edge, apart. The rest of us stand clear.",
      ironpalm: "The dance after the moons, with one word turned about. I did not set my feet near the far edge for it. The carriers put their moons short of that edge, in the dark half, apart, and I let them work.",
      ranger: "Moons plus a flipped cut. The flipped cut hits the far rim, so a moon parked there goes wide. Carriers parked theirs short of it, in the dark half, apart. I stood well off both.",
      shadowblade: "Moons and a turned cut: the turned cut strikes the far shadow, where a sensible moon would be left, and a lit moon bursts wide. So the carriers dropped theirs a little nearer the middle, in the dark half, apart.",
      pyromancer: "Moons AND a dance with a turned-over cut, how generous! The turned cut struck the far dark rim, so a moon left there went off wide. The carriers set theirs in the dark half, short of the rim, apart, and I stayed well back.",
      thermist: "Cold band, hot rim: the flipped cut strikes the far rim, so a moon dropped there is lit and bursts wide. The carriers set theirs one stride short of it, apart. I stood off them both, close to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Moons and a dance with a turned cut, and the turned cut hit the far dark rim, so the carriers ran their moons to the dark half but not as far as the rim, apart from each other, and I stayed well off them, because lit moons burst wide.",
      alembist: "Cause: moons set, then a dance with one inverted cut that strikes the far dark edge. Result: a moon left there is lit and bursts wide. Cure: the carriers stop short, in the dark half, apart, and everyone else keeps off them.",
      threadweaver: "Moons, then a dance with a turned cut. The turned cut takes the far edge, so a moon on it is lit and bursts wide. I told my carriers to stop short, in the dark half, and apart, and tied the rest of my stitches clear.",
      marionettist: "Props, then a dance with one cue played backwards. That cue strikes the far dark wing, so a prop moon left there is lit and goes off wide. The carriers placed theirs in the dark half, short of the wing, apart. The company kept clear.",
      cleric: "I asked the two carriers to stop a little short of the far edge, in the dark half, and to stay apart. The turned cut would strike that edge, and a moon lit by it would have burst wide. The rest of us kept off them, gently.",
      grovekeeper: "A turned cut in the dance after the moons, and it took the far dark edge like a late frost takes the low field. A moon left there would have been lit and gone wide. The carriers set theirs further in, in the dark half, apart.",
      chronomancer: "Moons, then a dance with a flipped cut, and the flipped one strikes the far dark edge, so a moon left there is lit and bursts wide. I will have sent the carriers short of the edge, apart, in the dark half; I did.",
      tidecaller: "Moons, and a dance with one cut turned over. The far dark rim took that cut, and a moon lit there would burst wide, so the carriers set theirs in the dark half, short of the rim, apart. We kept well clear of them.",
      lanternkeeper: "The turned cut lit the far dark edge, so no moon could sit there. The carriers brought theirs in short of it, in the dark half, apart, and I kept the lamp between the two. A moon in the light bursts wide.",
      kenseier: "One stroke of the dance is turned about and lands on the far dark edge. A moon left there is lit and bursts wide, so the carriers stopped short of the edge, apart, in the dark half. The rest of us stood off them.",
      houndmaster: "Flipped cut hits the far dark rim, so a moon left out there goes off wide. Carriers parked theirs short of the rim, apart, in the dark half. Dogs and I stood off, and watched the lot go up.",
    } },
    'The Second Howl': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Midway through the last dance the old howl came again, and a shade of the hound ran up the road. One of us wore the mark. The rest kept off the painted road and its end, and the marked one stepped clear before the landing.",
      gloomsworn: "The hound came back as a shade, which I understood; some things refuse to stay down. Mid-dance, one of us took the mark, and the shade ran their tracks in from the rim. We stood off the road, and the marked one left where it ended.",
      thresher: "Halfway through the dance, dear, the old hound's shade came running, hunting the steps of whoever wore the mark. We kept off the painted road, and our marked friend waltzed away from where it ended before he landed there.",
      bloodreaver: "The hound's ghost came back in the middle of the dance and I nearly cheered! He ran the marked one's road from the rim and pounced where it ended. I kept off his road, and the marked one stepped away from the end.",
      runeguard: "Margin note: in the middle of the dance a shade of the hound runs the road of the marked hero, from the rim to where they stood, and falls there. The rest of us stayed off the road, the marked one stepped away, and the dance went on.",
      gorgemaw: "A course nobody ordered arrived in the middle of the dance: the hound's shade, running the marked guest's footsteps from the door to their seat, then pouncing on it. We kept off the carpet it ran along. The marked guest left the chair.",
      oathbroker: "A rider to the terms, mid-dance: the hound's shade runs the road of the party marked overhead, rim to resting place, and lands there. The remaining parties stayed off the road and the landing. The marked party vacated once the road was set.",
      ironpalm: "A shade of the hound came running mid-dance. One of us wore the mark, and the shade ran that one's path from the rim and landed at its end. I stayed off the road. The marked one walked away and let it land on nothing.",
      ranger: "Mid-dance, the hound's shade. Runs the marked hero's trail from the rim, lands where it ends, and the dance keeps cutting the whole time. I stayed off the red road, watched the crown, and the marked one stepped off the end once it locked.",
      shadowblade: "The hound came back as a shadow, and I felt a small professional jealousy. Mid-dance he ran a road in from the rim, laid down by whoever wore the mark. I walked the safe ground between the cuts, well clear of it, and watched the marked one slip out from under the landing.",
      pyromancer: "Halfway through the dance the big hound came back as a shade, and a mark lit over somebody else's head! He ran their road from the rim and pounced at its end. I stayed off the red road, and the marked one leapt clear of the end.",
      thermist: "Reading in the middle of the dance: a shade of the hound, running the road of whoever wears the mark, rim to end, and pouncing there. The road ran cold, the landing ran hot. The rest of us kept off both, and the marked one left the landing.",
      driftblade: "The hound's shade came running mid-dance down the marked one's own road from the rim, and nobody was near it but the one who wore the mark, who was already away from the end, and the dance cut on without a pause.",
      alembist: "Cause: a shade of the hound in the middle of the dance, running the marked hero's own road from the rim. Effect: a red lane, and a landing at its end. Cure: everyone else off the lane, and the marked one off the landing.",
      threadweaver: "The hound's shade ran the marked one's path like a thread drawn through cloth, from the rim to where they stood, and then it pounced on the knot. I kept my stitches off the lane, and the marked one stepped out of the knot before it fell.",
      marionettist: "A cue nobody rehearsed: mid-dance, the hound's shade enters from the rim and plays the marked actor's own blocking back at them, ending with a pounce on their last mark. The company stayed off the path, and the marked one stepped off their mark.",
      cleric: "The poor hound came back as a shade in the middle of the dance, and ran the marked friend's road from the rim to where they stood. I asked the rest to keep off that road, and the marked one to step away from the end once it was drawn.",
      grovekeeper: "Mid-dance a shade of the hound came running, like frost down the marked one's own row, from the rim to where they stood. The rest of my lot kept out of that row, and the marked one stepped aside before it came down on the spot.",
      chronomancer: "The shade came up the road in the middle of the dance, and it ran the marked one's own past: every step they had taken lately, laid out red, ending where they stood. We stayed off that old road, and they left the place where it ended.",
      tidecaller: "A shade of the hound ran in from the rim mid-dance like surf along a shore, following the marked one's own wake to the place they stood. We kept off the wake, and the marked one swam clear of the end before it broke.",
      lanternkeeper: "A pale shade of the hound came up the road mid-dance, running the marked one's own steps like a lamp carried along them, and landing where they ended. I kept the others off the lit road, and the marked one left the last circle.",
      kenseier: "Midway through the dance a shade of the hound ran the marked one's road from the rim and fell at its end. I stood off the road, and the marked one stepped away from the end after it was drawn. The dance went on, and so did I.",
      houndmaster: "The old lad's shade, middle of the dance, running the marked one's trail from the rim like a hound on a line, and landing where it ends. Dogs and I stayed off the trail. Marked one stepped away from the end once it was set.",
    } },
  },
};
